tasks/BACKLOG.md:722:🟡 **F-2272-1 (s2272, non-blocking, OWNER'S DESK)** — the shim ships two gates nothing calls (`npm:test:codex-shim`, `server/codex-shim/serve.test.mjs`). Grandfathered in `scripts/gate-caller-baseline.json` with the measurement, on the `npm:test:asset-diet` / `halo-reextraction-check` precedent — but for a **stronger** reason than either: rooting it would spend **~16.7k prompt tokens of the owner's Codex allowance on every drain** (§7.3 makes spend an owner call), and its live arm depends on a third-party service, so an outage would red every unrelated drain (F-1460-1). **Stated plainly: this IS a partial coverage hole** — 2 of the 4 tests are pure (no auth, no network, 15 ms) and one, *"browser-origin requests cannot spend subscription quota"*, is a real security assertion. **RECOMMENDATION: split the file** — pure arms rooted, live arms owner-gated. GATE: owner's word on the spend.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:860:🔎 **F-2202-1 (s2202 2026-08-22, fire-side, MEASURED NOT CURED — F-2201-2's "HOW BIG IS IT? UNMEASURED" ANSWERED ON A DRY BOARD, AND THE ANSWER CHANGES WHERE A CURE WOULD HAVE TO AIM. GATE: none — this row is a measurement.)** 📐 **THE QUESTION, TAKEN FROM F-2201-2 VERBATIM: "for each reached `scripts/` path, is its discovering edge an INVOCATION site or a mention?" — one pass over the existing graph, no new instrument.** ⚠️ **FIRST, THE QUESTION NEEDED SHARPENING, AND THE ORIGINAL FORM IS UNANSWERABLE: `via` IS BFS-ORDER-DEPENDENT.** `gate-caller-audit.mjs:340` records whichever source happened to reach a node FIRST, so "the discovering edge" is an artifact of traversal order, not a property of the graph. Worse, the mention-only framing misses the actual failure: **`asset-diet.mjs` has TWO invocation edges AND a mention edge** — F-2201-2's own subject would not appear in a mention-only list. ➡️ **The class that can actually bite is: a subject carrying ≥1 MENTION edge, whatever else it carries — because a mention survives the subject leaving every battery it belongs to.** 📊 **MEASURED ON THE LIVE GRAPH: 158 subjects, 128 of them `scripts/` paths and reached; 134 edge occurrences into them — 114 INVOCATION · 20 MENTION. SEVEN subjects carry ≥1 live mention edge (7/128 = 5.5%). MENTION-ONLY: ZERO.** 🎯 **THE DOMINANT SOURCE IS NOT CONFIG READS — IT IS GUARD TEST FILES' OWN FIXTURES, WHICH IS THE RESULT I DID NOT EXPECT AND THE ONE THAT MATTERS: 5 of the 7 are shielded by a `*.test.mjs` fixture string, and FOUR of those five by `scripts/gate-caller-audit.test.mjs` ALONE** (`run-guards.mjs` ×3, `task-guard-audit.mjs`, `test-standings.mjs`, `asset-diet.mjs`), with `battery-manifest.test.mjs` shielding `gr-sim.test.mjs` ×2. The remaining two are data reads (`drain-block-check.mjs` ← `goal-closure-reason.test.mjs:34`+`law-pointer-guard.mjs:86`, both `readFileSync`) and one prose string (`assert-release-build.mjs` ← `gate-battery.mjs:143`). **A guard's test file MUST name real script paths in its synthetic fixtures to be realistic — and the edge vocabulary cannot tell a fixture from a caller. So the audit's own test suite is the largest single source of false reachability FOR the audit.** 🔒 **PROVEN BY MANUFACTURING THE DEFECT, WITH A CONTROL THAT BITES (the s1299/s1300 standard, and s2201's Attempt-1 lesson applied deliberately).** `test:stats` is literally `node scripts/test-stats.mjs && node scripts/test-standings.mjs` — **two subjects, one npm script, differing ONLY in whether a test fixture happens to name them.** ONE mutation in a detached worktree (`test:stats` → `echo cut-for-experiment`) removes BOTH invocation edges: **`scripts/test-stats.mjs` → `NO CALLER`, orphans 8 → 9, `FAIL — 1 gate(s) with no caller`, rc=1 (the control bites). `scripts/test-standings.mjs` → SILENT, and `--report` names its survivor edge: `via scripts/gate-caller-audit.test.mjs`** — a fixture string at `:637`. Harness controlled first: the same auditor via `--root` on the unmutated worktree reproduced main exactly (158 subjects / 8 orphans / PASS), and main was re-verified untouched afterwards. ⚠️ **THREE OF MY OWN PROBE'S BUGS ARE RECORDED BECAUSE EACH PRODUCED AN EVIDENCE-SHAPED FALSE ANSWER, AND THE THIRD WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A WHOLLY FICTIONAL FINDING.** **(1)** A proximity window read `node run-node-guards.mjs a.test.mjs b.test.mjs …` rosters as mentions — every entry after the first is invoked. **(2)** Line attribution was off by up to 10 lines: `stripComments`'s `/^\s*\/\/.*$/gm` lets **`\s` match NEWLINES**, so a blank line preceding a `//` comment is swallowed with it (944 → 934 lines on `gate-caller-audit.test.mjs`). ⓘ **Harmless in the audit itself — it collects a path SET and never reads a line number — but fatal to any probe that quotes lines; use `[ \t]` instead.** **(3) THE EXPENSIVE ONE: I scanned every `scripts/*.mjs` as an edge source. The audit does NOT.** `:335` reads a `.mjs` body **only if that file is itself REACHED**, while `:315` scans every `scripts/*.sh` unconditionally as a root. So mentions inside unreached `tmp-s<N>-*.mjs` scratch helpers are **INERT — not edges at all**. Before that fix the census read **17 exposed subjects with alarming entries** (`suite-red-inventory.test.mjs` "protected" by three s1655 scratch prose strings, `ruling-propagation-guard.mjs` by one); after it, **13, and every scratch entry vanished.** 💡 **A reachability probe must inherit its target's SOURCE SET, not just its regexes — I had copied `edgesOf` verbatim and still measured a different graph.** Two further apparent mentions were **variable-indirection invocations** (`const HARNESS = 'scripts/run-node-guards.mjs'` → `spawnSync(execPath, [HARNESS, …])`; same for `claimed-spec-harness-guard`), and four were **self-mentions, which are structurally inert** — a file's text is scanned only once it is already reached, so it can never bootstrap itself. **Each was settled by READING the file, not by the classifier.** 🚫 **NOT CURED, AND F-2201-2'S RESTRAINT IS STRENGTHENED RATHER THAN OVERTURNED — but the aim point has moved.** F-2201-2 predicted tightening would risk false orphans across the graph; the measurement says the exposure is **narrow (7 subjects) and CONCENTRATED (one file explains four)**, so a cure need not touch the vocabulary at all. **The cheap direction is now visible: the audit already PERSISTS `batterySteps` in `gate-caller-baseline.json` (s2201) precisely because deriving subjecthood from the live graph is vacuous — the same persistence answers this, by recording which edges are INVOCATIONS at the moment they are observed rather than re-deriving them from text.** That is a design question and an attended call, not a drive-by: it changes what green means for 128 subjects. ⚖️ **What is NOT owed is a new instrument** — the census re-runs from `scripts/tmp-s2202-edge-semantics.mjs` (banked, cited, unreached and therefore inert by the very property it measures). 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, and it is the fifth consecutive turn of the s2198→s2201 screw: every previous turn found a MEMBERSHIP test that was narrower than its definition. This one is the mirror image — an EDGE test that is WIDER than its definition, and it is wide in the direction that manufactures GREENS.** A narrow subject test loses gates silently; a wide edge test keeps dead gates looking alive. **Both fail toward silence, which is why neither was noticed for hundreds of fires — and why the test files a factory writes to guard its guards are exactly where the next such gap will live.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:862:🟢 **F-2201-1 (s2201 2026-08-22, FOUND AND CURED THE SAME FIRE ON A DRY BOARD — THE SUBJECT-ADMISSION SWEEP'S FOURTH TURN, ASKED EXACTLY WHERE s2199 AND s2200 POINTED. GATE: none — this row is a completion.)** 🔎 **THE DEFECT: A STEP OF A BATTERY WAS NOT A SUBJECT, BECAUSE ITS NAME DID NOT SAY "GUARD".** s2199 and s2200 each handed forward the same unread question — `GUARDISH_FILE`'s six words are still the admission rule for `scripts/*.mjs`, and the non-guard-named scripts have never been read one by one. **I read them, and the answer is again one layer over from the prediction.** 📊 **THE CENSUS, RE-DERIVED NOT INHERITED (s2200 carried "159"; the true figure is 154):** of **415** tracked `scripts/*.{mjs,sh}` — **100** TEST_FILE · **130** `tmp-s<N>-` scratch · **31** guard-named · **154 admitted by nothing**. 🎯 **THE SHARP QUESTION IS NOT "how many are unadmitted" BUT "which unadmitted file RUNS INSIDE A BATTERY", because that is where a dropped step becomes silence.** Walked across **all 25 gate-shaped npm scripts and their ~130 direct steps: EXACTLY ONE** is not a subject — **`scripts/asset-diet.mjs`**, a step of both `build` and `build:release`, which **throws on six separate budget/contract violations** (the Herald 1.5 MB spot-cut and dev-art ceilings, the exact 14-derivative roster, stale PNG references) and runs inside `npm run build` — **part of EVERY drain's gate battery per `fire.md` §3.** ⚖️ **ITS SIBLING STEP IN THE SAME NPM SCRIPT, `assert-release-build.mjs`, IS a subject — purely because its name contains "assert".** Two steps of one chain, one guarded and one invisible, separated by vocabulary alone. ⚠️ **THE PROOF TOOK THREE ATTEMPTS AND THE FIRST TWO WERE BOTH INVALID WHILE LOOKING EXACTLY LIKE EVIDENCE — recorded in full because that is the transferable part.** **ATTEMPT 1:** dropped `assert-release-build.mjs` from `build:release` as a control → **SILENT**, which reads as "the audit is blind to this too" but is nothing of the kind: **four other callers still reached it.** A control that cannot bite discriminates nothing. **ATTEMPT 2:** dropped `asset-diet.mjs` from both npm steps and read the silence as proof it was unreachable. **It was not unreachable** — `vite.config.ts:24` **READS the file to hash it into a cache key**, and the edge vocabulary counts that mention as a call. My in-degree probe reported "2 callers" because **it never walked `*.config.ts`** — the same class of blind denominator this whole sweep is about, in my own instrument. **VALID EXPERIMENT:** remove EVERY edge in the vocabulary — both npm steps AND the config read — then run the identical tree through pre-cure and post-cure source: **PRE-CURE rc=0, orphans 8, `asset-diet` named nowhere. POST-CURE rc=1, orphans 9, `FAIL — 1 gate(s) with no caller` naming it.** Guard-named control (`check-power-graph-budget.mjs`, in-degree 1, cut from its only caller) → **rc=1, orphans 8 → 9, in both.** ✅ **CURED — AND THE OBVIOUS CURE IS VACUOUS, WHICH IS THE LOAD-BEARING HALF.** "Admit what a battery calls" derives subjecthood from the CURRENT graph, so a file would be admitted **exactly when it has a caller** and could therefore **never be an orphan**: cut the edge and it stops being a subject in the same breath, and the audit falls silent again. **So the set is PERSISTED** in `gate-caller-baseline.json` under **`batterySteps`** — once a file has been a step it stays a subject until someone records why it should not be, and the writer is **ADDITIVE by construction** (a writer that rebuilt it from today's `package.json` would drop the entry together with the edge, restoring the exact silence, and would destroy the record besides — the RETENTION LAW, F-2199-2's lesson applied to a second set). 📊 **RESULT ON THE LIVE TREE: subjects 157 → 158, orphans 8 → 8, ZERO fresh — green before and after, so no red was buried.** It admits exactly **one** net new subject and **cannot flood**: `GATE_NAME` still decides which npm scripts contribute steps, so a human tool's helper (`dev`, `census`) never enters. **Baseline change purely additive: `grandfathered` and `superseded` byte-identical, `batterySteps` 118 entries seeded.** 🔒 **TEETH PROVEN BY MANUFACTURING THE DEFECT, NEVER BY A GREEN (the s1299/s1300 standard) — AND THE FIRST TEETH RUN WAS ITSELF FALSE, WHICH I CAUGHT ONLY BY SANITY-CHECKING AN UNTOUCHED TEST.** Staging the pre-cure audit into a scratch dir made **all five new arms "fail"**, which is exactly the result I wanted to see — but an **untouched pre-existing test failed there too**, because `law-surfaces.mjs` had never been copied and every run died on `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND`. **A harness that fails for the wrong reason produces a proof-shaped artifact and no proof.** Repaired, the honest result stands: **5 substantive arms fail pre-cure and pass post-cure; the CONTROL arm ("named by nothing, persisted nowhere → invisible") passes in BOTH, as an unchanged-behaviour control must.** **`gate-caller-audit.test.mjs` 38 → 45, 45 pass / 0 fail.** ⚖️ **SCOPE: this changes what the audit ADMITS. It wires no new battery leaf, moves no battery, and leaves F-1300-4's ordering law untouched. Attended review welcome — reverse with one word.** 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, and it is the fourth consecutive turn of one screw: s2198 found a membership test LEXICAL where its definition was BEHAVIOURAL; s2199 found admission by FILE EXTENSION under it; s2200 found the identical disease on the EDGE side of the same graph; s2201 finds that the definition the header states — *"a gate is something whose whole purpose is to return a verdict"* — was never implemented at all. Every class was a proxy for it. When a guard's header states a BEHAVIOURAL definition and its code tests a NAME, the gap is not a bug in the pattern — the pattern is standing in for a question nobody has asked directly. Ask what the repo already treats as a gate, and read that as the definition.** 📌 **NEXT SUBJECT ON THIS AXIS: `reached` means MENTIONED, not RUN — see F-2201-2, filed with this row.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:864:🔎 **F-2201-2 (s2201 2026-08-22, fire-side, NON-BLOCKING — A LIMIT RECORDED RATHER THAN QUIETLY FIXED, BECAUSE FIXING IT IS A DESIGN QUESTION AND NOT A DRIVE-BY).** ⚠️ **`gate-caller-audit`'s `reached` means "mentioned by something in the edge vocabulary", NOT "run by a battery" — and the two came apart on the very file that produced F-2201-1.** `vite.config.ts:24` does `readFileSync(resolve(process.cwd(), 'scripts/asset-diet.mjs'))` to **hash the file into a cache key**. It never executes it. But `edgesOf` matches any `scripts/[...].{mjs,sh,ts}` mention in a config, so that read is an EDGE. 📐 **THE CONSEQUENCE, MEASURED: with F-2201-1's cure in place, cutting `asset-diet.mjs` out of `build` AND `build:release` STILL leaves it green** — the config's hash-read alone keeps it "reached", so a gate can leave every battery it belongs to and the audit will still report a caller. **F-2201-1 closes the SUBJECTHOOD hole (the file can now orphan at all, which it previously could not) and explicitly does not claim to close this one.** 🚫 **NOT CURED HERE, DELIBERATELY, AND THE RESTRAINT IS THE POINT.** Distinguishing a MENTION from an INVOCATION is not a regex tweak: the same vocabulary that would have to stop counting `readFileSync(...)` is what correctly counts a `webServer` command, a `node --test` roster entry, and a `bash scripts/foo.sh` line — and the audit's own test file already pins *"a guard named only in a COMMENT is not reached — prose is not a caller"*, i.e. this instrument has drawn this exact line once before and drew it by stripping comments, which cannot help here because the read is live code. **Tightening it risks manufacturing false orphans across 187 reached script paths, which is how a ratchet decays into a thing fires excuse (F-1460-1's `cross-engine` fate).** ⓘ **HOW BIG IS IT? UNMEASURED, and that is the honest state — I found the class by tripping over one instance, not by enumerating it.** The cheap first question for whoever picks this up: **for each reached `scripts/` path, is its discovering edge an INVOCATION site or a mention?** That is a one-pass classification over the existing graph and needs no new instrument. 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF: a reachability graph inherits the semantics of its edge vocabulary, and "X appears in Y" is a much weaker claim than "Y runs X". A guard built on the first while reasoning about the second is right almost always — and wrong precisely where a file is read as DATA by the same tooling that elsewhere runs it as CODE.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:866:🟢 **F-2200-1 (s2200 2026-08-22, FOUND AND CURED THE SAME FIRE ON A DRY BOARD — THE SUBJECT-ADMISSION SWEEP'S THIRD INSTRUMENT, ASKED EXACTLY WHERE s2199 POINTED. GATE: none — this row is a completion.)** 🔎 **THE DEFECT: `gate-caller-audit` ADMITS TEST FILES BY SUFFIX, SO A TEST NAMED WITH A HYPHEN WAS NOT A SUBJECT AT ALL.** s2199 handed forward `GUARDISH_FILE` as the next thing to ask and named 66 unread non-guard-named scripts. **I asked, and the answer was one line over from its prediction — again, which is now the pattern this sweep should expect rather than a surprise.** `GUARDISH_FILE` itself is sound; the hole is in the class beside it. `:200` built the test-file class as `.test.mjs || .test.sh` — **admission by SUFFIX — so the seven tracked `test-<thing>.{mjs,sh}` files matched NEITHER that nor `GUARDISH_FILE`, and were excluded from the subject set by construction.** 📊 **MEASURED s2200: six of the seven run inside a gate-shaped battery.** `test:stats` is literally `node scripts/test-stats.mjs && node scripts/test-standings.mjs`; `test:accounts` and `test:mp` are each a single such file end to end; `test:node-guards` chains `test-ticker-stats.mjs` and `ticker-stats.mjs`; `build:release` chains `asset-diet.mjs`. ⚠️ **THE COST, PROVEN AGAINST A PRE-CURE CONTROL RATHER THAN ARGUED: I deleted `node scripts/test-standings.mjs` from `test:stats` and ran the audit AS IT STOOD AT HEAD — rc=0, subjects 151, orphans 8, zero fresh, output BYTE-IDENTICAL to the intact board. Total silence.** That is F-2199-1's exact failure mode one layer on, and F-1252-2's under that. 🔑 **AND THE SAME BLINDNESS SAT ON THE EDGE SIDE, WHICH IS THE HALF THAT MADE THIS MORE THAN A ONE-LINE FIX: subjects were admitted by SUFFIX, edges by LOCATION.** `edgesOf` matched `scripts/[...].{mjs,sh,ts}` plus `.test.mjs` anywhere — so a test file living outside `scripts/` **could not be reached however loudly a battery called it**. Curing only the subject side would have manufactured an orphan that no rooting could clear. **A half-cure control test now locks that in: with the subject widening alone, the fixture reds as an unclearable orphan.** ✅ **CURED both sides at one shared `TEST_FILE` predicate.** 📊 **RESULT: subjects 151 → 157, reached 218 → 219, orphans 8 → 8 with ZERO fresh — green before and after, so no red was buried.** The widening admits 6 net new subjects (two of the seven already matched `GUARDISH_FILE` on the word "contract"; the test class wins, so they are not double-counted) and mints **exactly ONE fresh orphan**. 🎯 **THAT ORPHAN IS A TRUE POSITIVE AND THE BEST THING THIS SWEEP HAS FOUND: `foundry/kit/test-init.sh` — the foundry kit's own gate, 82 assertions over the whole scaffold contract (tree, template instantiation, plist lint, config schema, both runners' `--dry-run`, the never-overwrite refusal), exiting nonzero on any failure. Its ONLY caller was a sentence in `kit-README.md` telling a human to run it.** 💰 **ROOTED in `test:ledger-guards`, not grandfathered, and the reason is measured: it is 82 passed / 0 failed / rc=0 in 0.7 s.** Every one of the 8 grandfathered orphans sits on the owner's desk on **COST** (`asset-diet` 52.3 s, `halo-reextraction-check` +28% per drain, `release-base` 18.7 s) — at 0.7 s, **1.3% of that battery's measured 53.4 s end-to-end (rc=0, all 19 leaves)**, that argument simply does not apply, and minting a 26th excuse for something cheap, green and genuinely unguarded is how a ratchet decays. ⚖️ **SCOPE: this wires ONE new leaf into `test:ledger-guards` and changes nothing about WHEN that battery runs; F-1300-4's ordering law is untouched. Attended review welcome — reverse with one word, it is a single `&&` clause in `package.json`.** 🔒 **TEETH PROVEN BY MANUFACTURING EACH DEFECT, NEVER BY A GREEN** (the s1299/s1300 standard): a `test-<thing>.mjs` nothing calls is **byte-invisible pre-cure and rc=1 after**; dropping one from a live chain now REDS; a test file outside `scripts/` is REACHED when cited and an unclearable ORPHAN under the half-cure; and a `test-` prefix in a DIRECTORY name admits nothing — the control against flooding. **`gate-caller-audit.test.mjs` 38/38 (was 34).** 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, and it is the third consecutive turn of the same screw: s2198 found a membership test LEXICAL where its definition was BEHAVIOURAL; s2199 found admission by FILE EXTENSION under it; s2200 finds the identical disease on the OTHER SIDE OF THE SAME GRAPH. When you cure a membership test, ask the mirrored question immediately — a graph has two sides, and the vocabulary that decides what is a SUBJECT is rarely the same code as the one that decides what is an EDGE. Curing one alone can be worse than curing neither, because it converts silence into a red nobody can clear.** 📌 **NEXT SUBJECT ON THIS AXIS, still unread: `GUARDISH_FILE`'s own six words remain the admission rule for `scripts/*.mjs`, and s2199's 66 non-guard-named scripts have still never been read one by one. That set is now 159 by my wider count and it is where a fourth turn would look.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:946:✅ **F-2117-1 SHIPPED — the dispatched line is EXECUTED AND DRAINED (s2160 authored, s2161 drained at `96e9236d4`; `reviews/f2117-1-release-base-harness.md`).** The only browser test of the shipped `/goldrush/` sub-path build — the configuration `deploy-alias.sh:14` maps to the live `goldrush-base` alias — **now has a runner, and the suite that had never once been run in this repo is GREEN**. Port freed `5191 → 5295` (an 18-day-old `vite preview` squats 5191 and answers `/goldrush/` with HTTP 200, so with `reuseExistingServer: false` the suite could not begin — the alias alone would have shipped a script that fails on first invocation, the trap s2117 refused). `test:release-base` added; grandfathered in `gate-caller-baseline.json` in the `npm:test:asset-diet` voice rather than rooted, because its `webServer` runs a full `build:release` — **gate policy, left for the owner**. 🔑 **THE ACCEPTANCE SIGNAL IS THE REPO'S OWN INSTRUMENT, re-measured on the MERGED tree, not inherited from the runner: `claimed-spec-harness-guard` WARN **2 of 3 → 1 of 3**, naming only `accounts-sync.spec.ts`** — computed by `deriveHarnessMap()` inside a file the master firewalled the runner out of touching. Also: `test:release-base` **2 passed**, desktop+mobile · `gate-caller-audit` **PASS, unrouted 0, grandfathered 24 → 25** · the 24 pre-existing baseline entries proven **byte-unchanged by parse-compare** (added 1, removed 0, changed 0), not by eyeballing · tsc clean · build green · all three paths LANE-TOUCHED/MAIN-UNMOVED, no graft. Gated in a **detached worktree** throughout (§3.0b) because a live attended session held main's tree for the fire's whole duration. 🔺 **F-2161-1 (NON-BLOCKING, and it changes an owner input):** the grandfather's cost citation of **18.73s** is a quiet-machine floor — the same suite measured **39.4s (2.1×)** on the merged tree at load 12.1. A drain gate runs exactly when the machine is busiest, so whoever carries **F-2159-1**'s gate-policy question to the owner should carry a **RANGE (~19s quiet / ~39s loaded)**, not a point estimate. The runner's figure was annotated, not edited: it is a correct measurement of what it measured. 🔺 **F-2161-2 (REPORT-ONLY, not a fire's to fix):** an uncommitted attended `goals.json` leaf `b4v4-picnic-admission` carries `mergeHash: 780b2ab9b…` — **this fire's LOCK commit, whose entire diff is `STATUS.md` 1 insertion 1 deletion**; the picnic content landed at `79d9a874e`. The *HEAD-splice-is-correct-exactly-once* shape: a helper stamping `mergeHash: <current HEAD>` is right only until a fire takes the lock in between. `goal-tracker.test.mjs` cannot catch it — the value is a valid 40-hex hash of a real commit, so only the **provenance** is wrong, not the schema.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1018:🚫 **THE MASTER DELIBERATELY DOES NOT ROOT IT, AND THE REFUSAL IS THE FACTORY'S OWN PRECEDENT APPLIED A FOURTH TIME.** The config's `webServer` runs a full `npm run build:release` (`tsc && vite build && asset-diet && assert-release-build`) before one assertion, so rooting it in a pre-merge battery is a permanent per-drain tax — **gate policy, owner's desk**, on the `halo-reextraction-check` (+28%), `npm:test:asset-diet` (52.3s) and s2159 ART-audit (+50%) dispositions. Instead the master grandfathers `npm:test:release-base` in `scripts/gate-caller-baseline.json` **in the `npm:test:asset-diet` voice**, because a new gate-shaped npm script with no caller would otherwise flip `gate-caller-audit` from **PASS / `unrouted: 0` / `grandfathered: 24`** to `NEW … NO CALLER` — **curing one orphan by minting another.** Acceptance is the repo's own instrument and not the runner's prose: `claimed-spec-harness-guard` prints **`WARN: 2 of 3`** today and must print **`WARN: 1 of 3`** naming only `accounts-sync.spec.ts`.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1021:🔺 **F-2159-1 (s2159 2026-08-22, fire-side, HALF CURED THIS FIRE + HALF ON THE OWNER'S DESK — F-2158-1'S PRICED RECOMMENDATION, MEASURED AND THEN SPLIT IN TWO BECAUSE THE MEASUREMENT CHANGED THE ANSWER. GATE: owner picks (a), (b) or (c) below for the GATE half; the DUTY half is landed and reversible with one word.)** 📋 **WHAT F-2158-1 ASKED FOR:** it filed the ART staging audit's trigger as the real finding — *"a duty gated on 'any fire that touches X' is structurally unobservable precisely when X is idle, and for retention, idleness is not the quiet period — it is the accrual period"* — and priced one recommendation two ways, deliberately not building either: **make the audit a standing dry-board duty**, *or* **root `art-staging-audit --strict` in `test:ledger-guards`**. It called the second *"a legitimate guard candidate where the gazette one was not"* because `AT RISK > 0` is a pure measurement, not a judgement, so it cannot decay into an excused label the way F-1460-1's `cross-engine` did. **That reasoning is sound and I did not refute it.** ⚖️ **WHAT IT DID NOT DO IS MEASURE THE COST, AND THE COST IS WHAT DECIDES WHO OWNS THE DECISION.** 💰 **MEASURED s2159 ON THE LIVE BOARD, BOTH SIDES:** `node scripts/art-staging-audit.mjs --strict` runs **31.9 s** (rc=0; 983 files across 5 areas) and `npm run test:ledger-guards` runs **63.7 s** (rc=0, green). Rooting the first inside the second is therefore **+50% on every fire's last act, permanently** — permanently because the 983 staging files stay on disk *by the Retention Law itself*, so the steady-state cost does not decay as the exposure is cured. 🔍 **WHY IT IS SLOW, since the number invites the obvious fix:** `art-staging-audit.mjs:110` and `:115` call `git hash-object` and `git cat-file -e` **once per file** — ~1,966 subprocess spawns. Batching (`--batch-check`, or hashing in-process) would very likely make this near-free. 🚫 **I DID NOT DO IT, AND THE REASON IS THE INSTRUMENT'S OWN HISTORY:** that is the exact code path that has **shipped a false zero TWICE** (F-1054-1 name-vs-blob, F-1055-1 local-vs-remote), and §2E's standing order for a third gap is *fix the class, not the instance*. **A false zero is the worst possible failure of a retention instrument** — it reports safety while history dies — so an optimisation there is a real fire's work with manufactured-defect proof, not a dry-board drive-by taken to dodge a policy question. ⚖️ **WHY THE GATE HALF IS NOT MINE TO TAKE — PRECEDENT, TWICE, IN THE FACTORY'S OWN WORDS.** `gate-caller-audit` (PASS, `unrouted: 0`, verified s2159) grandfathers `scripts/halo-reextraction-check.mjs` with exactly this disposition: *"Wiring it costs 45.3s on top of test:node-guards' 162.6s (+28% on every src-touching drain), **which is gate policy and not a drive-by: owner's desk**, on the npm:test:asset-diet precedent (52.3s, same disposition)."* **My change is +50% where that one was +28%**, so a fire rooting it unilaterally would be overruling a standing owner-desk disposition on a strictly larger cost. ⓘ **AND IT IS NOT A COVERAGE HOLE TODAY:** `scripts/salvage-art-staging-reach.test.mjs` is already in the battery, but it asks *does the CURE reach the exposure?* against scratch repos — **it never asks the live board whether anything is at risk right now**, which is genuinely the uncovered question. So the gate would buy real coverage; it is the price, not the value, that is in dispute. ✅ **THE HALF I DID LAND, BECAUSE IT COSTS NOTHING AND CLOSES THE SAME HOLE: `scripts/fire.md` §2E ART-SLOT LAW now triggers on a DRY BOARD as well as on touching the slot.** A dry board is the condition that actually recurs — it was the condition of this fire and of the four before it — whereas the ART slot has been untouched for weeks. At **fire scope** 31.9 s against a 35–50 min drain budget is free; at **gate scope** the same 31.9 s is the disputed +50%. **Same instrument, same measurement, opposite verdict, purely because of where it is invoked** — which is the reusable half of this finding: *a cost is not a property of a tool, it is a property of the tool's CALLER, and the same number can be trivial and prohibitive in two places one line apart.* 🔺 **OWNER'S DESK — one word, on the GATE half only (the duty half is already landed and reverses with one word if you dislike it):** **(a) RECOMMENDED — root it, but batch the subprocess spawns FIRST**, so the gate is near-free and the +50% question never has to be answered; costs one small master with manufactured-defect proof on an instrument with a false-zero history, which is why it wants a real fire rather than this one. **(b) root `art-staging-audit --strict` in `test:ledger-guards` as-is** — coverage today, +50% on every fire's last act forever. **(c) leave it at the dry-board duty landed here** — zero cost, catches accrual within one dry fire instead of within one fire, and leaves the live board unmeasured on the (rare) stretch where no fire is ever dry. ⓘ **This same word would sensibly settle `halo-reextraction-check` (45.3 s) and `npm:test:asset-diet` (52.3 s), which have sat on the desk on identical reasoning** — three items, one policy: *what is a battery second worth?* 📊 **BOARD STATE THIS FIRE, for the record:** `AT RISK 0 / LOCAL-ONLY 0` — s2158's salvage (`save/art-staging-20260822`) holds and its push landed; the remaining 983 staging files classify **760 SALVAGED / 22 DIVERGED / 201 SHIPPED**, all bytes in an object database. **Nothing is at risk as I write this**, which is exactly the moment to fix the trigger rather than the bytes.
