tasks/BACKLOG.md:1546:✅ **f1623-1-town-transfer-determinism** `tasks/lane-f1623-1-town-transfer-determinism.md` (lane-a, QUEUED s1623) — FIRE-AUTHORED from **F-1623-1** above, closing its gate verbatim. Adds a settled stopping point (no in-flight same-origin request for 1500 ms, 20 s cap) in place of the racing `assetLoadingState === "ready"` flip, splits every arm into total / unique / duplicate bytes, and proves stability across **three consecutive runs** into a new `artifacts/asset-diet/town-transfer-stability.md`. Test-only; `src/assets/AdvanceStream.ts` is READ-ONLY because it is the subject. **A null is pre-authorised — REFUTED with the best actual percentage satisfies the gate; a widened tolerance or a dropped run does not.** ✅ **SHIPPED s1625 `9e4b09c09`** — merged from lane/a at 2/6 on its own suite: **all four reds are `expect(…).toBeLessThan(TOWN_TRANSFER_CEILING_BYTES)` at `:226`/`:399`**, the class the master predicted and pre-authorised, **zero behavioural failures**, drain run reproducing the runner headline exactly. Review `reviews/f1623-1-town-transfer-determinism.md`. **Two findings filed — F-1625-2 (release-gate breakage) and F-1625-3 (shell-local stability) — and the corrective is DISPATCHED to lane-c in the same fire, which is the condition of the verdict rather than a courtesy (F-1460-1).**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1551:✅ **F-1620-7 (s1620 2026-08-10, CLOSED s1623, VERIFIED AT THE f1619-2 DRAIN BY READING BOTH SITES — A BUDGET CEILING GUARDED BY TWO DISAGREEING INSTRUMENTS IS A GUARD THAT WILL EVENTUALLY BE BELIEVED AT THE WRONG NUMBER.)** ⚙️ `e2e/asset-diet.spec.ts` asserts `townResponseBytes < 25_000_000` in **two** tests — the pre-existing throttled-cues test at `:103` and the new A/B test at `:225`/`:226`. Both name the quantity `townResponseBytes`. **Their values differ by 9.5 MB on mobile:** the pre-existing test reports **12,932,580** (12,067,420 headroom) while the new normal arm reports **22,469,519** (**2,530,481 headroom**). ➡️ **The project's belief about how much room it has under the ceiling depends on WHICH TEST YOU READ, and the tighter number is the new one — 2.5 MB of real headroom, not 12 MB.** ? **INFERRED, and flagged as inference rather than measured:** the older test throttles GLBs by design, so its total is plausibly a *partial* load captured inside the measurement window — but the drain did NOT verify that, and **the two must not be reconciled by assumption.** ⚠️ **CONSEQUENCE FOR THE QUESTION f1619-2 WAS AUTHORED TO SETTLE:** f1615-1's disputed figures (desktop `21,389,200 → 16,209,181`, mobile `15,181,572 → 17,074,995`) sit **between the two instruments' scales**, so the unverified **~1.9 MB "growth" may itself be partly an instrument mismatch rather than a real regression.** 💡 **This slice therefore does NOT close f1615-1 — it establishes that closing it requires naming the instrument first**, which is a better outcome than a delta number that would only have invited a story. 📈 **STRENGTHENED s1620, MEASURED BY THIS FIRE’S OWN DEPLOY — IT IS THREE INSTRUMENTS, NOT TWO, AND THE THIRD IS THE ONE THAT GATES RELEASES.** `scripts/deploy.sh` runs its own **first-town asset budget** check against the **same 25,000,000 ceiling** — its printed headroom sums to exactly 25,000,000, so the ceiling is identical, not merely similar — and reported **desktop 16,174,837 (8,825,163 headroom) · mobile 15,218,288 (9,781,712 headroom)** on the very build published as `891edb2c3`. ⚠️ **So MOBILE now has three different answers to one question: 12,932,580 (throttled-cues test) · 15,218,288 (deploy gate) · 22,469,519 (the new A/B normal arm) — a spread of 9.5 MB against a 25 MB ceiling.** 🎯 **This is what raises the finding from tidy-up to load-bearing: the deploy figure is the one that can BLOCK A RELEASE, and it is the second-most optimistic of the three.** If the A/B arm is the honest measure of what a traveller actually pulls, the release gate is passing builds with **~2.5 MB** of real headroom while reporting **~9.8 MB**. ℹ️ **Stated as the open question it is, not as a verdict:** the three may legitimately measure different scopes (throttled partial load · built-bundle static budget · full navigated walk), and **this fire did NOT establish which is right** — only that three named instruments answer one named ceiling differently and nothing reconciles them. **GATE: none owed to the owner. Closes when the two `townResponseBytes` sites either measure the same thing or are renamed so they cannot be confused, AND f1615-1's figures are restated against a named instrument. RECOMMEND doing this BEFORE any further asset-diet tuning — a 2.5 MB real headroom is a very different planning constraint from 12 MB.** ✅ **CLOSED s1623 2026-08-10 by `181e1835b` (f1621-1, review `reviews/f1621-1-town-budget-instrument-reconciliation.md`) — BOTH limbs of the gate satisfied.** The ceiling is now one exported `TOWN_TRANSFER_CEILING_BYTES` in place of four bare literals; the two quantities are renamed apart (`cueWindowResponseBytes` vs `normalBytes`) so they can no longer be confused; f1615-1s figures are restated against the **throttled-cues / cue-window instrument**. ⚠️ **But read F-1623-1 before you use ANY number in this row:** every figure quoted above is a single draw from an instrument the drain has since measured as unstable by up to **3.08x at fixed configuration**, so the "9.5 MB spread between three instruments" is now known to be of the same order as the noise of ONE of them. The reconciliation this row asked for is *named*, not yet *achieved*.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1560:ⓘ **F-1620-8 (s1620 2026-08-10, OBSERVED AT THE f1619-2 DRAIN — NON-BLOCKING): desktop and mobile disagree on the `saveData` delta by 37%, and the reason is sitting unread in the artifact.** Drain run: desktop delta **4,029,556** vs mobile **2,943,858**. Both arms exclude the same two bulk halls (**2,584,796** bytes), so the ~1.1 MB difference comes from the *other* thing `saveData` does — narrowing advance-stream prefetch to `priority === 1` — which evidently removes more on desktop than on mobile. **The per-URL tables contain the answer; nobody has read it out.** **GATE: none owed. Worth one pass before the delta is quoted anywhere.** ✅ **ANSWERED s1621 BY READING THE COMMITTED ARTIFACT — AND THE SIGN REVERSES, SO THE FINDING'S FRAMING IS REFUTED RATHER THAN CONFIRMED.** ✓ VERIFIED by reading `artifacts/asset-diet/town-budget-desktop-chrome.md` and `…-mobile-chrome.md` on main. ⚠️ **First, an identification the finding did not make: the committed artifact is the RUNNER's run, not the drain's** — its mobile normal reads `22,110,931`, which `reviews/f1619-2-asset-diet-town-budget-ab.md` names as the runner figure against the drain's `22,469,519`. So the drain's per-URL tables, which F-1620-8 quotes totals from, **were never committed and cannot be decomposed by anyone now.** 📐 **In the run that IS on disk, both deltas decompose EXACTLY, to the byte:** desktop `2,584,802` = the two bulk halls (`dynamo-hall` 1,407,064 + `stamp-mill` 1,177,732 = **2,584,796**) + 6 B of `.js` shim noise; mobile `2,910,139` = the same 2,584,796 + **eight `char-hero-sheet-walkdiag8-r2c*` PNG sprite sheets totalling 323,909 B** + 1,434 B of shim noise. Nothing is unaccounted for in either. ➡️ **THEREFORE THE ANSWER TO "what does the `priority === 1` narrowing remove?" IS: on mobile it removes eight hero walk-sheet PNGs (323,909 B); on desktop it removes NOTHING beyond the halls** — those PNGs do not appear in the desktop table at all, meaning both desktop arms treated them identically. ⚠️ **This is the OPPOSITE of the finding's premise.** F-1620-8 reasoned from `desktop 4,029,556 > mobile 2,943,858` that the narrowing "removes more on desktop than on mobile"; in the committed run **mobile is the larger delta** (`2,910,139 > 2,584,802`, by exactly the 325,337 B those PNGs and the shims account for). 🔎 **The reconciling fact, and it points at an instrument rather than a behaviour: mobile is STABLE across the two runs (2,910,139 vs 2,943,858, 1.2% apart) while DESKTOP swings 2,584,802 → 4,029,556, a 1.44 MB / 56% move on the same build.** 💡 **That is F-1620-3's instability, in a second column** — F-1620-3 already measured the desktop `Wire bytes` column run-to-run unstable by up to 3×. **So F-1620-8 is best read as one sample of an unstable desktop quantity, not as a desktop-vs-mobile behavioural difference, and the 37% figure must NOT be quoted as a platform fact.** ⚠️ **What is NOT established:** why desktop is the unstable side, and whether the drain-run desktop figure would decompose the same way — unanswerable without its per-URL table. **GATE REVISED: the "read the tables" duty is DISCHARGED. What remains is a stability question, which belongs to F-1620-3, not here — and the practical rule stands: quote neither delta as a platform difference until desktop is shown to reproduce.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1576:🔎 **F-1619-1 (s1619 2026-08-10, MEASURED — THE SWEPT CANDIDATE LIST s1618 LEFT FOR THE NEXT FIRE IS 6/6 ALREADY SHIPPED, AND THE PROPERTY IT WAS SWEPT ON CANNOT DETECT THAT.)** ⚙️ s1618 ended a dry board by handing forward *"the expensive half: a swept candidate list"* of never-ran `lane-*` masters **"with a LIVE first line"** — i.e. lacking the ⛔ DO-NOT-QUEUE header the s1130–s1132 sweeps added by hand. ✓ **All six were stale-checked this fire and every one is already on main:** `lane-a-perf-05-startup-attribution` (drained `63161278` s1039 — verified by FILE PROBE, not by a leaf: the `hasAny` needle in `e2e/perf-05-startup.spec.ts` now reads `/icon-` **with** the leading slash, which is precisely the cure that master proposed, so the substring collision with `favicon-32.png` it was authored to fix no longer exists) · `lane-c-ed-04-gizmo-guards-vacuous` **shipped** · `lane-resource-timing-false-greens` **merged** · `lane-m2-01-fixture-coordinate` **merged** · `lane-d-rf-03b-specs-supersession` **merged** · `lane-b-asset-diet-budget-runnable` **shipped** (corroborated independently of its leaf: `package.json` carries `test:asset-diet` running `e2e/asset-diet.spec.ts` under the preview config — the runnable budget check that master was written to create). ⚠️ **THE REUSABLE HALF IS WHY THE SWEEP READ CLEAN: the absence of a DO-NOT-QUEUE header is SILENCE, NOT A NEGATIVE.** That header is added by hand, only when someone happens to notice a shipped master — so it is present on a biased sample and absent from everything nobody has looked at. **Sweeping on it selects for un-audited masters and calls them available**, which is Mistake #8’s exact intake path (the 824k flail was a re-derivation of an already-merged diff). 💡 **The one-command check already exists and costs a second: `node scripts/drain-block-check.mjs <master>` resolves the goal leaf and PRINTS `status="shipped"` / `"merged"`.** ⚠️ **But READ THE WORD, NEVER THE TICK — it exits `0` with a green `✅ CLEAR` for a shipped leaf**, because its job is block-checking, not shipped-ness; a fire branching on the exit code alone reads *already merged* as *cleared for dispatch*. That is F-1262-3’s lesson (rc=0 UNKNOWN reads as permission) recurring on a different field of the same tool’s output. ➡️ **THEREFORE: a candidate list is worth handing forward only if each item was resolved through its goal leaf AND one file probe; a list swept on header-absence should be labelled UNAUDITED, not CANDIDATE.** ⓘ **Stated in s1618’s favour, because that handoff was honest and its reasoning was sound on the evidence it had:** it flagged the list as *"worth a stale-check, NOT a blind queue"*, and its ⭐ pick (`asset-diet-budget-runnable`) was chosen for the right reason — a live thread pointed at it. **That thread is real and is now taken up by `f1619-2`, which measures the budget the shipped check made measurable.** **GATE: none owed to the owner. Closes when a future dry-board sweep resolves its candidates by leaf + probe rather than by header-absence; the fire that does so should retire this line.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1700:✅ **F-1565-1 (s1565 2026-08-08 — CLOSED s1568, CURED AND MERGED at `661e13d9b34e2270eeb99fbf19ee3fc4d0fe35bc`. A REFUSAL NOW OUTLIVES THE FIRST IDLE SURVEY, SO THE PLAYER IS TOLD THEY WERE REFUSED).** ➡️ **CLOSURE, s1568:** `ProspectorEmbodiment.say()` takes the receipt's typed voice kind, and a `refusal` raises the existing absolute survey deadline to at least `simulationAt + Balance.agent.refusalHoldSeconds` (**8** — clears the 7 s first-survey deadline with a second of reading margin, well under the 19 s idle cadence). The kind is threaded from `voiceKindForReceipt`, so **the refusal vocabulary is not duplicated outside `Voice.ts`** (F-1261-1) — the master's main design constraint. The slice chose the `nextSurveyAt` shape over a new `lastRefusalAt` field, reusing one timer invariant rather than adding state the survey branch must consult. ✓ **THE RED WAS RE-MANUFACTURED AT THE DRAIN, NOT INHERITED** — a passing test never executes its violation path. Removing **only** the three-line hold on the merged tree: **rc=1, `1 failed`, and it is exactly `e2e/m4-06-embodiment.spec.ts:432` ("permission-denied bark survives the first idle survey")**; restored byte-identically (`git status` and `git diff HEAD` both clean). Matches the runner's claimed before-state (`2 failed`, both projects, `Expected value: "ledger"`). 📌 **The suite had documented this defect and routed around it; `e2e/m4-06-embodiment.spec.ts:426-427` ("permission-denied receipts do not send the Prospector to the denied target") — its F-1558-1 comment declining to assert `after.lastLine` is now false and was REMOVED — the master named that as part of the deliverable.** ⚖️ **FIREWALL HELD: neither `0.4` drift constant moved** (no numeric `expect` argument changed; gate observed driftAbs 0.2834 desktop / 0.3722 mobile, gapClosed 0.2823 / 0.3714, inside unchanged bounds), and the accepted refusal set was **not** widened to admit `ledger` — the cure the master forbade. 🧾 **Gates (merged tree, detached worktree `gate-s1568` per §3.0b):** tsc rc=0 · build green (vite 1.03 s, asset-diet 1,158,214/1,500,000 B) · **m4-06 spec 10/10 desktop + 10/10 mobile at `--workers=1`** · **`test:node-guards` rc=0, 393 tests / 390 pass / 0 fail / 3 skipped** (292 s) · all chained leaves PASS. ⓘ **Run on Node v26.4.0, matching `.nvmrc`** — the runner's report records an earlier ambient-Node-23.11.1 invocation returning 393/390/3 whose two failures are F-1507-1's file-granularity `--test-timeout` semantics; it re-ran under the pinned Node **instead of re-pinning a test** (F-1441-3), which is the right call and is recorded here so nobody reads that number as a regression. Review: `reviews/f1567-2-refusal-bark-hold.md`. ⓘ **NON-BLOCKING, checked rather than assumed:** `handleReceipt` went from `if (line)` to `if (line && kind)`, which reads like a narrowing that could suppress barks. It cannot — ✓ verified by reading `src/agent/Voice.ts:42-49`, `barkForReceipt` returns `null` whenever `voiceKindForReceipt` is falsy, so `line` truthy implies `kind` truthy; the only null kind is `get_state`, already early-returned at `Embodiment.ts:115`. The conjunct is inert, not narrowing. **Original finding preserved below.** 🟡 **(as filed s1565):** THE DENIAL BARK CAN BE SILENTLY OVERWRITTEN BY IDLE CHATTER, AND IT IS THE GAME THAT IS WRONG, NOT THE TEST).** ✓ **TRACED, not inferred** (f1564-1 scope-6, `reviews/f1564-1-m4-06-ceiling.md`): the receipt route is SYNCHRONOUS — `AgentStub.panAt` calls `record` (`src/agent/AgentStub.ts:86-89`), which invokes its listeners synchronously (`:134-146`), which calls `handleReceipt` (`src/game/Game.ts:2221-2223`). `barkForReceipt` classifies a failed non-`NO_SYSTEM_API` receipt as `refusal` (`src/agent/Voice.ts:36-48`), whose vocabulary is held / ask me / no trust (`:13`). The word `ledger` belongs to the `survey` vocabulary (`:14`) and is reachable ONLY via the idle-survey route in `updateSimulation` (`src/agent/Embodiment.ts:126-139`); there is exactly ONE writer, `say` (`:315-316`). ➡️ **So an observed `ledger` immediately after a PERMISSION_DENIED means the refusal bark was written and then OVERWRITTEN before the assertion read it. The accepted refusal set is CORRECT; the race is the bug.** A denied player can have the denial silently replaced by idle chatter. ➡️ **CURE: make the refusal bark sticky for a short floor, or have the survey timer refuse to overwrite a refusal within N seconds** — small and localised in `Embodiment.ts`. ⚠️ `src/agent/` is NOT one of F-1460-1’s three trigger paths, but this moves behaviour the sim replays: **run `test:node-guards` anyway.** **GATE: none — fire-authorable.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1713:✅ **F-1566-3 (s1566 2026-08-08 — CLOSED s1567, CURED AND MERGED at `3d4651f91175b30dc57d19db46e407b411e69abf`. THE GUARD BUILT TO STOP SILENT DESK DROPS COULD BE SATISFIED BY A CITATION; ITS CLOSURE EXIT NOW ASKS THE SUBJECT-FIRST READER).** ➡️ **CLOSURE, s1567:** `desk-carryforward-guard`'s closure exit calls `subjectLedClosure(backlogText, id)`, exported from `scripts/desk-state-audit.mjs` by lifting `classifyFinding()`'s first half into a shared `subjectState()` — **one implementation of "subject-first", not two** (F-1261-1), and **`scan()` itself is untouched**, as is the `wide` vocabulary and the `DESK-DROPPED:` exit. 4 files, +143/−5; `findings-state-guard.mjs` and `package.json` both absent from the diff, the two firewall violations the master named as task failures even under a green suite. ✓ **THE RED WAS VERIFIED AT THE DRAIN, NOT INHERITED** — a passing guard never executes its violation path, so its green is not evidence about the red (s1299/s1300). Restoring **only** the old predicate and its import on the merged tree: **rc=1, exactly 1 of 15 tests fails and it is the new one** (`✖ citation-only closure cannot silently excuse a dropped desk item`); with the cure, **15/15**; the file restored byte-identical. ✓ **AND PROVED AGAINST THE LIVE LEDGER, not only the fixture:** `subjectLedClosure(backlog, 'F-1541-2')` → `[]` (no longer read as closed) while its citing row's real subject `subjectLedClosure(backlog, 'F-1542-1')` → `[91]`. 📐 **THE LIVE-BOARD BEFORE/AFTER THE MASTER MADE AN ACCEPTANCE CONDITION: PASS → PASS, identical** (s1566's real handoff board via `git show fc7c38d96:STATUS.md` against main's BACKLOG; previous desk s1565 22 items, this desk 22, **dropped 0**). **No flip — and the honest reason is that with zero drops the closure exit was never reached**, which is consistent with, but does not independently confirm, the zero-exposure claim. The cure landed while latent, which was the whole argument for doing it now. 🧾 **Gates (merged tree, detached worktree `gate-s1567` per §3.0b):** tsc rc=0 · build green (vite 1.03 s, asset-diet 1,158,214/1,500,000 B) · **`test:ledger-guards` 129/129 pass, 0 fail** (baseline 124/124) · **12/12 chained leaves**. No Playwright and no `test:node-guards` owed — no `src/**`, no `e2e/**`. Review: `reviews/f1566-3-desk-guard-subject-first.md`. **Original finding preserved below.** 🟡 **(as filed s1566):** `desk-carryforward-guard` lets a dropped desk item pass on two grounds: an explicit `DESK-DROPPED:` clause, or BACKLOG recording the id closed — the second implemented at `scripts/desk-carryforward-guard.mjs:201–203` as `!closed.get(id)?.closed.length` against `scan(backlog, {closedVocabulary:'wide'})`. ⚠️ **`scan()` attributes a row's state to EVERY F-ID inside that row's 90-char subject zone, not only to the row's subject** — so an id merely CITED by another finding's closure row reads as closed, and the guard will let it leave the desk in silence. 📊 **MEASURED s1566 on the live ledger: the wide census calls 343 ids closed and 21 of them (6.1%) have NO subject-led closure row at all** — `F-1541-2` (closed@85, that row's subject is `F-1542-1`), `F-1264-3` (@959, subject `F-1264-1`), `F-1176-1` (@1337, subject `F-1175-3`), `F-1200-2` (@1356, subject `F-1200-3`), plus `F-1271-5 F-1252-3 F-1252-2 F-1201-2 F-1152-3 F-1154-3 F-1150-4 F-1060-2 F-1079-6 F-1081-8 F-1130-4 F-1137-2 F-1222-3 F-1449-1 F-1467-1 F-1464-1 F-1259-2`. ⚖️ **THIS CORRECTS A DIRECTION THE LAW ASSERTS.** F-1561-5 says this guard *"fails SAFE (over-strict), never open"*. True of the `OPEN-DESK-ONLY` half; **false of the closure exit**, which fails OPEN. A guard that cries wolf gets routed around (F-1460-1); a guard that fails open gets TRUSTED, which is worse. ✅ **EXPOSURE TODAY IS ZERO and must not be read as reassurance:** none of the 21 appears among the 22 items s1565 carried, and `desk-state-audit` reports `CLOSED=0` across the whole live desk. Latent, not live — fix it before the coinci