tasks/BACKLOG.md:1863:🟢 **s1532 NEGATIVE RESULT, RECORDED BECAUSE IT COST A FIRE AND SHOULD NOT COST ANOTHER: `lane-advance-stream.md` IS NOT AN UNREGISTERED MASTER, AND A FILENAME-KEYED LEDGER CHECK IS THE WRONG INSTRUMENT.** Sweeping never-ran masters, this fire flagged `tasks/lane-advance-stream.md` as the one candidate with a BACKLOG line but **no ship marker** — its stated predecessor (`lane-asset-diet`) is shipped, and its own gate (*"probe the cue's dataset exists on main, else STOP"*) **PASSES**, so every signal read QUEUEABLE. That is a Mistake #8 landmine shape. ✗ **It was a false alarm, and the reason is reusable:** the ledger records the ship **in prose, under the work's NAME, not its FILENAME** — BACKLOG:2170 reads *"✅ THE ADVANCE STREAM + THE CASCADE DRAINED (s1026 fire, `e109639f`)"*. Grepping the **filename** returned only the stale 2160 CORRECTION line that predates the merge. ✓ **Confirmed by reading main rather than by a second grep:** `src/assets/AdvanceStream.ts` (253 lines, merged `e109639f8`, lane-c, `perf:` prefix, title identical to the master) implements every scope item — priority tiers ①–④ (`advanceStreamPriority`), `requestIdleCallback` batching, abort-on-transition, `saveData` collapse to tier-1, the `assetPrefetchProgress` dataset seam **consumed by the raise-cue at `src/assets/AssetLoading.ts:53`**, and the cascade frontier (last-suspended → last-played → next-unbeaten); `e2e/advance-stream.spec.ts` exists. ➡️ **RULE: check a master's shipped-ness by the ledger's PROSE NAME and by a file-probe on main — never by grepping its filename.** The Completeness Law is satisfied here; the ledger was right and the instrument was wrong.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1925:🟡 **F-1515-1 (s1515 — THE f1501-5 CURE IS A BACKSTOP, NOT A CURE: GREEDY DELIMITER CONSUMPTION SURVIVES IT, AND A CLASS IT CANNOT COVER WAS MEASURED.** Non-blocking, fire-authorable — **but it must be PRICED before it is authored.**) The f1501-5 master offered the union as *"a PROVEN-SAFE FLOOR, not the required implementation"* and named the cleaner alternative: *"a non-destructive scan that enumerates all candidate spans instead of consuming them greedily … is arguably what the code meant to do all along."* The runner took the floor. **That is within licence and the slice merits its merge** — but the underlying design is intact, and the union masks the defect only where a *same-kind* pairing happens to survive the greedy walk. 📐 **MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED** — replaying both regexes from the merged guard over three windows: title · short code span · title → loose ✗, by-kind ✓, **union RECOVERS**; title · bare apostrophe · title → loose ✗, by-kind ✓, **union RECOVERS**; **an ODD count of same-kind quotes before the title → loose ✗, by-kind ✗, union does NOT recover.** `he said "foo and then some prose "<title>".` yields `"foo and then some prose "` from **both** arms and never the title, because an unbalanced same-kind delimiter earlier in the window makes both scanners consume the wrong pair. ⚠️ **PRE-EXISTING, NOT INTRODUCED:** the merged change is a measured strict superset of main (`263/205/43 → 262/206/43`, nothing lost), so the residual was already there and the slice strictly improves on it. It fails **CLOSED**, so it costs cycles rather than shipping defects. 📊 **PRICED THE SAME FIRE — INCIDENCE IS 2, NOT ZERO, SO THIS IS NOT THEORY (measured s1515, method below).** Copied the guard, dropped its CLI at `:293`, re-rooted it and widened `rows.push` to carry `win` + `titles`. **CONTROL FIRST:** the copy reproduces the shipped guard exactly — **511 / 262 / 206 / 43** — so it is the same instrument and the answer is about the corpus, not the copy. An all-pairs non-destructive scan then recovers **4** `NUMBER-ONLY` rows — **but 2 of those 4 are artefacts of [F-1515-2] and are NOT test titles at all** (`e10Static.arrivalZ`, cited from `tasks/lane-rig-repair.md:14` and `tasks/DRAFT-rehearsal-rig-repair-and-rerun.md:14`). ✅ **The 2 genuine ones were verified by reading the spec, not by trusting the matcher:** `e2e/tl-01-run-telemetry.spec.ts:229` *("plain no-debug secure return keeps telemetry invisible to gameplay")* and `e2e/asset-diet.spec.ts:73` *("honest town and claim cues appear while GLBs are throttled and leave at ready")*. ⓘ **2 is a LOWER BOUND:** my matcher required `MIN_PREFIX` on the `title.startsWith(span)` branch where the guard's `matchesATitle` imposes no minimum and also has fragment logic, so the shipped matcher is *more* permissive than the probe. **A 2-citation cure is small; the case for building it is that it also removes the workaround tax — prose has been hand-massaged around this bug for 13 fires.** 🪤 **AND THE OBVIOUS WAY TO MEASURE IT SILENTLY RETURNS A FALSE ZERO — this is the reusable half.** 🪤 **AND THE OBVIOUS WAY TO MEASURE IT SILENTLY RETURNS A FALSE ZERO — this is the reusable half.** `scan()` is exported, but its rows are `{file, raw, spec, verdict, carried, mdLine, context}`: there is **no `window` and no `titles` field**. A probe filtering on those (as s1515's first attempt did) skips **every** row and reports `0 recoverable`, which reads exactly like *"the residual never occurs"*. It was caught only because `scripts/citation-title-guard.mjs` has **no `import.meta.main` guard** — importing it executes the CLI and calls `process.exit()`, so the probe **died loudly instead of lying quietly**. **REC: the pricing duty is DISCHARGED — build it, together with [F-1515-2], which lives in the same file and whose cure removes 2 of the 4 raw hits. GATE: closes when the odd-same-kind-delimiter window is recovered by a manufactured-defect arm proved RED against today's union scanner, with `citations == 511` and `CARRIES-TITLE >= 206` held.** Related: [F-1501-5], [F-1514-1], [F-1506-2]. ✅ **SHIPPED s1516 (`8b853308dea09a5d6d8ed553cf91cf7de342a360`), leaf `merged`, review `reviews/f1515-1-citation-scan-nondestructive.md`.** `matchingQuote()` now enumerates **every** same-kind delimiter pair in the window **without consuming them**, so the odd-same-kind-delimiter class this row measured is genuinely cured rather than backstopped. Gated in detached worktree `gate-s1516` (§3.0b): `tsc` rc=0 · `test:node-guards` **rc=0, 351 tests / 348 pass / 0 fail / 3 skipped** at Node 26.4.0 · `--report` **515 / 259 / 212 / 44** against main's **515 / 262 / 210 / 43** (**CARRIES-TITLE +2, NUMBER-ONLY −3, denominator unmoved, both tables summing**). Manufactured-defect arm **RE-PROVED at the drain, not inherited** — reverting only the guard to main's blob reds it by name (`an odd same-kind delimiter cannot consume the following quoted title`, 18 tests / 16 pass / 2 fail), restored byte-identically. ⚠️ **AND THIS ROW'S OWN GATE HAD ALREADY ROTTED, WHICH IS [F-1515-3] LANDING A SECOND TIME IN ITS OWN LEDGER:** the gate says *"with `citations == 511` and `CARRIES-TITLE >= 206` held"*, but the corpus was **515 / 262 / 210 / 43** before a line of this cure was written — the absolute numbers were stale by 4 citations the moment later fires edited `tasks/**`. The bar was met in the **RELATIVE** form the re-authored master used (baseline taken by the runner itself, `scanned` equal · `CARRIES-TITLE ≥` · `NUMBER-ONLY ≤`), which is the form that cannot rot. **Any future gate naming an absolute corpus count is writing its own expiry date.** 💡 Attempt 1 STOPPED correctly on exactly this hazard and cost no lane time beyond the pre-flight.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1999:- 🟠 **F-1489-3 (s1489, MEASURED — THE DEPLOY-TIME ASSET BUDGET GATE HAS BEEN FAILING ON EVERY DEPLOY SINCE 01:18 TODAY, AND IT FAILS AS A *WARNING*, SO NOTHING STOPPED.)** Found by not waving at a WARN line: this fire’s deploy printed `[deploy] WARN: asset budget check failed — default mode continues` and I checked whether I had caused it, because the slice I had just shipped **doubles the byte size of 74 sprite files** and this is the deploy-time gate over exactly that. ✅ **Not mine, and not a budget overage at all** — `logs/deploy.log` records the real cause: `Error: F-1457-1: nothing is answering at http://127.0.0.1:5188`. `scripts/deploy.sh:59-64` runs `e2e/asset-diet.spec.ts` with **`GR_CAPTURE_EXTERNAL_SERVER=1`**, which makes `scripts/external-server-guard.mjs` (the `globalSetup`) demand an already-running server — and **the deploy script starts none**. The guard is doing its job correctly and refusing early; the caller is wrong. 📉 **THE MEASUREMENT THAT MAKES IT A FINDING RATHER THAN A SHRUG:** across the whole of `logs/deploy.log` there are **132 real budget measurements and 6 failures — and all 6 are dated 2026-08-06** (01:18, 02:30, 03:20, 03:45, 05:03, 16:21). Before today this gate measured every deploy; since 01:18 today it has measured **none**, while every deploy still reported success. ⚠️ **The blast radius is that `BUDGET_OVER` can no longer be reached**: the `while read` loop at `:65-72` is fed by a `sed` over output that now contains no `townResponses` line, so a genuine budget overage would be indistinguishable from this harness failure — both land on the same WARN, and `STRICT` mode is off by default. **GATE: closes when `scripts/deploy.sh` either starts a preview/dev server before the budget spec or drops `GR_CAPTURE_EXTERNAL_SERVER=1` so the spec starts its own, and a deploy log again shows an `asset budget <project>: <n> bytes` line.** ℹ️ Worth pairing with the review note in `reviews/f1486-1.md`: the halo cure spends real headroom (`asset-diet` 1,158,214 of 1,500,000 at build time), so this gate matters more now than it did yesterday. ✅ **CURE SHIPPED s1490, commit `2d5ed1a94` — and the ROOT CAUSE is one level deeper than the finding stated, which is the part worth keeping.** The finding read the flag as simply wrong; it was in fact **inert and harmless for all 132 measurements**, because `playwright.preview.config.ts` overrides `webServer` with its own `:5189` server and nothing ever listened on `:5188` for the flag to mean. What changed is `2474c51ac` (2026-08-05 22:43) wiring `external-server-guard` in as `globalSetup` **on the BASE config** — and the preview config is `{ ...baseConfig, webServer: <own> }`, so **the spread carries `globalSetup` across**. Overriding `webServer` does not override it. The dormant flag was armed by a change to a file it does not name. ⚖️ **Cure per the GATE's second option:** the flag is dropped, so `deploy.sh` now matches `npm run test:asset-diet`, which has always run without it. 📊 **Both arms measured, same box, same `dist/`, `--workers=1`: ARM 1 (flag) rc=1, 0 measurements, `F-1457-1` refused · ARM 2 (no flag) rc=0, `desktop-chrome` and `mobile-chrome` both `townResponses: 20,100,070 bytes`.** Against the 25,000,000 limit that is **4,899,930 bytes headroom, 80.4% consumed** — so the gate was **blind, not lenient**; no overage had slipped through in the six blind deploys. ➕ **Closed one better than the GATE asked, on the finding's own blast-radius paragraph:** zero parsed projects is now its own failure with its own words (`MEASURED NOTHING: asset budget parsed 0 projects`) instead of sharing a WARN with a genuine overage, and `STRICT` mode now ABORTs on it — **which shuts a hole that predated the guard entirely**, since a playwright run exiting 0 while emitting no `townResponses` line would previously have passed even in strict mode. Proved on the violation path rather than the green (s1299/s1300 standard) by executing the **shipped** block against synthetic captures: real lines → 2 measurements, `BUDGET_OVER=0`, no false alarm; empty capture → `MEASURED NOTHING` + WARN; `STRICT=1` with rc=0 and zero measurements → `ABORT`, `finish budget_failed 5`. ⚠️ **ONE CONJUNCT OF THE GATE IS DELIBERATELY LEFT OPEN AND MUST NOT BE READ AS DONE:** the GATE also asks that *"a deploy log again shows an `asset budget <project>: <n> bytes` line"*, and **this fire ran no deploy** — it merged no gameplay-affecting code, and the DEPLOY LAW has bookkeeping fires skip deploy. The equivalent line was produced twice off-deploy (ARM 2's raw measurement, and the shipped parser emitting `asset budget desktop-chrome: 20100070 bytes (4899930 bytes headroom)`), but that is not the same evidence. **GATE REMAINDER: the next fire that merges gameplay code and deploys confirms the line appears in `logs/deploy.log` — expect ≈20.1 MB of 25 MB — and only then is F-1489-3 fully closed.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:2015:- 🔺 **F-1473-2 (s1473, MEASURED — THREE GATE-COST DECISIONS DECLARE THEMSELVES “OWNER’S DESK” INSIDE A JSON REASON STRING, AND NOTHING ROUTES THEM THERE; ONE OF THE THREE HAS NO LEDGER ROW AT ALL).** 🧭 **HOW I FOUND IT:** s1472 listed “root `halo-reextraction-check.mjs`” as next-fire priority (B) with an inherited **recommendation YES**. Re-pricing it before adopting it, I read the entry it came from — and the entry’s own last sentences say wiring it **“is gate policy and not a drive-by: owner’s desk, on the npm:test:asset-diet precedent”**. So the recommendation a fire was being told to *execute* is one its own source reserves for **Robin**. Adopting it would have been a fire making a gate-cost ruling on the owner’s behalf. 🔍 **THE MEASUREMENT, over all 9 entries in `scripts/gate-caller-baseline.json`: THREE claim the owner’s desk** — `npm:test:asset-diet` (F-1253-1, +52.3s), `npm:test:release` (F-1253-1 / F-1126-1, ~200s on a 124s battery), and `scripts/halo-reextraction-check.mjs` (F-1470-3, +45.3s on 162.6s, **+28% on every src-touching drain**). ⚠️ **AND `F-1470-3` APPEARS ZERO TIMES IN THIS FILE** (`grep -c` = 0), so it exists ONLY as prose inside a JSON value: no BACKLOG row, no blocker-panel row, no desk line, invisible to `findings-state-guard`, `blocker-panel-closed-guard` and `desk-declaration-guard` alike. F-1253-1 and F-1126-1 at least have rows (5 and 9), but **none of the three has ever been on a desk** — the live desk carried 8 items at s1472 and none of them is this. 🎯 **WHY IT COULD NOT SURFACE, which is the reusable half:** `gate-caller-audit.mjs:311-314` only tests **`s.key in baseline`** — key PRESENCE. The reason string is printed and **never parsed**, so writing “owner’s desk” into it is a **no-op that reads like an escalation**. The audit PASSes, the fire feels it discharged the duty, and the owner is never asked. This is F-1334-2’s class (26% of desk items not on the board the owner reads) **one level further out**: not