/assets/`). Merge: base `dfed3765`, **zero MAIN-MOVED files**, all 15 paths byte-identical to the branch. Leaf `gg-03e-herald-class-map-engravings` → `shipped`, `note_s1256` retired into six drainNotes. *(Superseded the s1255 QUEUED line.)*
tasks/BACKLOG.md:2662:🔺 **F-1261-2 (s1261, MEASURED — A SECOND, INDEPENDENT DATUM FOR THE F-1252-1 OWNER QUESTION, AND THIS ONE HAS NAMES).** The citation ratchet **failed my own strike text** on first run: 4 citations cited a line with no recoverable test title — `e2e/bt-00-demolish.spec.ts:121` ("demolish refunds full-HP palisade and frees its footprint for replacement"), `e2e/m2-01-build-menu.spec.ts:340` ("stress draw calls stay under 200 with palisades and beacons"), `e2e/asset-diet.spec.ts:15`, `e2e/release-build.spec.ts:48` ("first player reaches textured town actors and places a Dry Gulch spring sluice"). Repaired by quoting each enclosing title, and `asset-diet.spec.ts:15` **has no enclosing test at all** — it is a file-level `test.skip(process.env.GR_ASSET_DIET_BUNDLE !== '1', BUILT_BUNDLE_ONLY)`, so it is now cited by symbol with the line dropped. ⚠️ **Two of the four (`m2-01:340`, `asset-diet:15`) I inherited verbatim from `artifacts/findings-state-vocabulary-triage.md`** — which means the triage report's own citations would have failed the ratchet had the report lived under `tasks/**`. **That is F-1260-7 reproduced independently, with four named citations instead of a count**, and it strengthens rather than settles the owner-scope question F-1252-1 poses: the ratchet's value is demonstrated precisely where it does not currently reach. Still owner-scope; **no corrective authored.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:2746:✅ **F-1256-2 CLOSED (s1257, by execution) — the flag was right, the drain acted on it, and the forbidden file was clean.** s1256 predicted the drain would have to ask *"what did rewriting seven working plates buy us"* rather than *"did the derivatives arrive"*, and to **check `scripts/asset-diet.mjs` first**. Both done. **Answer: nothing measurable.** All seven re-encodes are **384×384 before and after** (webp headers parsed, main vs lane), sizes moved in both directions, net **+13,794 B** on the seven plus a legitimate 44,514 B for the eighth. Budgets never touched. So the false premise cost churn, not correctness — recorded as F-1257-1 below rather than as a separate thread. 💡 *The durable half is that **the flag paid for itself twice**: once by telling the drain which file to open first, and once by telling it which question the master could not ask.*
tasks/BACKLOG.md:2766:🚨 ~~**F-1256-2 — ✅ CLOSED, see L50 (closed by execution at the GG-03e drain); struck s1261 as measured STALE by `artifacts/findings-state-vocabulary-triage.md` (s1260, `637e156e`) — ✓ RE-DERIVED s1261 BY CODE PROBE: merge `5e129079`; `src/news/heraldReader.ts:15-23` maps all eight engravings including `ceremony`, all eight `assets/processed/herald-engraving-*.webp` exist on disk, and `scripts/asset-diet.mjs` still enforces the same ceilings. Reasoning retained per the Retention Law.** **F-1256-2 (s1256, MEASURED — READ BEFORE DRAINING GG-03e) — THE MASTER I WATCHED RUN THIS FIRE WAS AUTHORED ON A "MEASURED, NOT INFERRED" FACT THAT IS FALSE, AND THE INSTRUMENT THAT PRODUCED IT WAS A GREP FOR THE WRONG WORD.** `tasks/lane-a-herald-class-map-engravings.md` (s1255-authored, ran lane-a 12:26–13:03, done-move waiting) rests on this, quoted from its own WHY and repeated in its goal leaf: *"the class map is **already written** … and **every one of its entries currently resolves to `undefined`**, because it globs `assets/processed/herald-engraving-*.webp` and **no file of that name exists**"* — with the proof command published inline as **`ls assets/processed/ | grep gazette`**. ➡️ **That command greps for `gazette` to answer a question about `herald-engraving`.** Re-run this fire (`logs/session-scratch/s1256/herald-premise-check.mjs`): **all seven `herald-engraving-{board,trail,river,schoolhouse,ledger,boss,town-growth}.webp` exist on disk with mtime `2026-07-29T06:18–06:20Z` and are TRACKED ON MAIN** (`git ls-tree main`), landed by `b5be7ab3` — **a full day before the claim was written**. The absence was a property of the search string, not of the tree. **So the Herald was NOT silently text-only for every class, and "the missing thing is the derivatives, not the map" is exactly backwards.** ✅ **The master was right about the genuinely missing piece:** the **eighth** class had no plate and `HeraldClass` had no `ceremony` member — seven members against the spec's eight — and the run added it. **This is not a no-op and must not be triaged as one.** ⚠️ **BUT THE DRAIN MUST ASK A DIFFERENT QUESTION THAN THE MASTER POSES.** Not *"did the seven derivatives arrive"* — they were already there — but *"what did rewriting seven working plates buy us"*: the run replaced all seven (`board` 36,904→42,198 · `boss` 44,702→43,668 · `schoolhouse` 48,116→31,566 — **sizes move in both directions, so this is a re-encode, not a compression pass**), and touched **`scripts/asset-diet.mjs` in 6 lines**, which the master **explicitly forbade in one direction** (*"do NOT raise either ceiling to make room"*, F-1253-2's lesson). **Check that file first.** The note is also written onto the `gg-03e-herald-class-map-engravings` leaf so `drain-block-check` surfaces it. 💡 *This is F-1255-5's lesson one turn further and pointed the other way. That fire found that an inherited NOTE can be confidently wrong; this one finds that **a fire's own freshly-measured fact can be too, when the measurement is a negative search.** A grep that returns nothing reports the vocabulary you typed, not the state of the world — and the fix is one line long: before publishing an absence, search for the thing by the name the CONSUMER uses. `heraldReader.ts` names those files literally, one per line; opening it would have refuted the claim instantly.*~~
tasks/BACKLOG.md:2876:🔎 **F-1251-1 (s1251, MEASURED, NOT FIXED — OWNER/ATTENDED CALL) — 8 OF THE 9 `release-build` ROWS IN THE KNOWN-RED INVENTORY ARE NOT BUGS: THEY ARE A CONFIG-OWNED SPEC BEING RUN BY THE WHOLE-SUITE HARNESS THAT CANNOT POSSIBLY SATISFY IT.** 🧩 **The mechanism, verified by reading both configs:** `playwright.config.ts` selects `testDir: './e2e'` and ignores only `**/*.rig.ts`, so **`e2e/release-build.spec.ts` is in the default whole-suite run** — where the webServer is `npm run dev` on 5188, a non-release build. Its assertions are about the E1 release door (`__GR_TEST__` undefined, later flagship URLs declining, debug seams inert, epoch clamping, an E1-only `dist/`). They cannot hold there. 🔬 **PROVED BY TWO ARMS ON ONE UNCHANGED TREE, NOT BY READING:** **ARM A** (`playwright.release.config.ts`, the spec's own harness) → **25/26 at default workers, 26/26 rc=0 at `--workers=1`** — reproducing s1246's measurement of the F-1180-2 load transient exactly. **ARM B** (same file, same commit, DEFAULT config) → **rc=1, 20 passed / 6 failed, 201 s** — and the six are three logical tests × both projects whose error strings match the committed inventory **verbatim**: *debug and era query seams are inert* → `Test timeout of 30000ms exceeded.` · *an imported later ledger heals to the frontier and plays* → `Error: expect(received).toBe(expected) // Object.is equality` · *later flagship URLs decline to the Claim* → `Test timeout of 30000ms exceeded.` (inventory rows at `logs/suite-red-inventory.md:192–200`). 📉 **ARM C closes the fourth title and exposes a second-order defect: that test is NOT HERMETIC.** *dist has no later manifest ids or plate/GLB assets* **passed** in ARM B — only because my own release run had just left a `GR_RELEASE=e1` `dist/` on disk. Rebuild with the plain `npm run build` (what `deploy.sh` runs) and the same test under the same config **fails with `expect(received).not.toThrow()`** — the inventory's exact signature — caused by `[release-build] later epoch manifest id in assets/Game-BueKHwMI-diet-abbb9d04.js`. **Same commit, same config, same test, opposite verdicts depending on leftover build state.** ➡️ **So of the 9 `release-build` rows in the failing table: 6 are harness-structural (proved), 2 are the stale-`dist` artifact (proved), and exactly 1 — *first player reaches textured town actors…*, MOBILE-ONLY, `build ghost z was -14, expected -15` — is a genuine gameplay red.** 🪞 **IT IS A CLASS, NOT AN INSTANCE:** `e2e/asset-diet.spec.ts` is also config-owned (it needs `GR_ASSET_DIET_BUNDLE=1` and the preview server on 5189 via `playwright.preview.config.ts`) and contributes **2 more rows** (`logs/suite-red-inventory.md:38–39`). There is **no `testIgnore` mention anywhere** in `tasks/BACKLOG.md`, `docs/`, `reviews/` or `scripts/` — this angle has not been examined before. 💥 **WHY IT COSTS SOMETHING:** these rows are permanent, they inflate the red count every fire reads, and a fire triaging them will re-measure a "known red" forever without ever being able to fix it. It is also how a *real* red hides — the genuine ghost-z row sits in the same block as eight impossible ones. 🔺 **NOT FIXED, DELIBERATELY:** the obvious remedy (add the spec to the default config's `testIgnore`) would also drop **20 currently-passing project-cases**, and the narrower remedy (skip the release-only tests unless the release harness is active) edits spec semantics. Both change the whole-suite baseline every fire compares against, which is a **gate-policy call, not a drive-by** (§7.3). Recommendation: exclude config-owned specs from the default harness *and* give `test:release` a real caller in the same change, since one without the other either loses coverage or keeps manufacturing noise. **Evidence: `logs/session-scratch/s1251/{test-release-run1,test-release-run2-workers1,armB-default-config,armC-stale-dist}.txt`.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:2880:✅ **F-1250-1 (s1250, MEASURED + FIXED THIS FIRE) — THE §3.0 GUARD PICKED THE WRONG LEAF: AN EXACT taskFile MATCH LOST TO A LONGER-NAMED SIBLING, SO ASKING ABOUT A TERMINAL-CLOSED LEAF COULD RETURN `✅ CLEAR` ABOUT A DIFFERENT ONE — AND THE BOARD'S OWN NAMING CONVENTION MANUFACTURES THE COLLISION.** Three fires running, the same guard has been wrong three different ways: s1248 fixed its *status vocabulary*, s1249 fixed its *reason vocabulary*, and this fire found that both were downstream of a *matcher* that could answer about a leaf nobody asked about. `findLeaves` sorted hits by `taskKey.length` alone (`b.taskKey.length - a.taskKey.length`) — a sound heuristic for the INEXACT case (a run-stamped done-move containing its taskFile) applied so broadly that it **overrode identity**. Successors on this board are named by appending a suffix, so a predecessor's key is ALWAYS a strict substring of its successor's: **all 4 collisions among the 227 live leaves are that shape** (`lane-asset-diet` ⊂ `lane-asset-diet-gate-honesty`; `lane-blocked-storage-boot` ⊂ `…-boot-2`; `lane-d-suite-red-inventory` ⊂ `…-run-tree-invariance`; `lane-calibrate-suite-workers` ⊂ `…-v2`). 🔬 **PROVED BY CONSTRUCTION AGAINST THE PRE-FIX SUBJECT, BOTH DIRECTIONS** — a superseded predecessor beside its longer merged successor printed **`✅ CLEAR — lane-blocked-storage-boot-2.md [blocked-storage-boot-2] status="merged"` at rc=0**, an affirmative clearance over a terminal-closed leaf naming a file the fire never asked about (F-1104-7's shape a third time, reached through the matcher instead of the status word); and the reverse — a lawful `merged` successor beside a longer `superseded` predecessor printed **`⛔ CLOSED` at rc=1**, reddening a legitimate drain. A guard that cries wolf is spent as surely as one that clears wrongly. 📊 **ZERO LIVE INSTANCES THE DAY IT LANDED, AND THAT IS THE POINT, NOT A LET-OFF.** All 227 leaves were probed **through the real script** (F-1249-1's lesson: run their instrument, not your model of it): 5 blocked and 15 terminal-closed, **every one rc=1**. It was latent, not academic — **3 of the 4 collision predecessors are `merged`/`shipped` TODAY, and the CLOSED refusal's own printed epilogue instructs retiring exactly such a leaf to `superseded` when a successor lands.** Following this guard's own advice was the single edit that would have armed it against itself. 🧩 **THE TELL WAS AN ASYMMETRY INSIDE ONE FUNCTION:** the BLOCKED arm scans **all** hits (`hits.find(l => l.status === 'blocked')`), the CLOSED arm judges **`hits[0]`** — so owner blocks were never exposed and closures always were. When one file decides the same question two ways, diff its own arms against each other. ✅ **FIXED:** an exact normalized match now sorts first and length only breaks ties among inexact hits — the rule the `--queue` arm already used at `:276` and the drain path never had. No duplicate normalized keys exist (227/227 distinct), so "exact" is unambiguous here. Both refusal arms now disclose the other matches, so a shadowed sibling is never invisible. 🧪 **4 NEW ARMS, EACH WITH A CONTROL, MUTATION-PROVED ONE MUTATION PER BRANCH** (subject restored byte-identical, sha256 `28c31df1981d1650`): **M1** revert to pure longest-wins → **3 red** (both exact-match arms + the rider) while the *"no exact match → longest still wins"* arm stayed **GREEN**, which is the control proving the tie-break was **preserved, not replaced** · **M2** drop the blocked-sibling disclosure → **1 red** · **M3** drop the CLOSED arm's `also matched` line → **1 red**. Gates: `tsc` rc=0 · `build` rc=0 15.5 s · **`test:node-guards` 128/128 (baseline re-measured at HEAD = 124, so +4 is exactly these arms)** · `test:task-guards` 747 masters / 0 invisible · `run-guards` **8/8** rc=0 122.3 s. 🔎 **RIDER — s1249's lead (I) is EXPLAINED AND PARTLY CLOSED, not cured.** `lane-calibrate-suite-workers.md` still resolves its *refusal* through the blocked `-v2` sibling, because the BLOCKED arm's all-hits scan is deliberate and **can only ever produce rc=1** — conservative and correct, so it stays. What was actually wrong was the *report*: the reader saw only the `-v2` owner block and never learned their own leaf was `superseded`. The refusal now prints `you asked about : calibrate-suite-workers status="superseded"`. Verified live. 📌 **ONE OBSERVATION LEFT DELIBERATELY UNACTED (not a finding, and not mine to decide):** `rf-19-blocked-storage-boot`, `rf-08-asset-diet` and `factory-suite-red-inventory` are `merged`/`shipped` while each has a landed successor; whether any should be retired to `superseded` needs their history read, and I did not read it. The matcher is now safe either way — which is the whole reason that question is no longer urgent.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:2894:📏 **MEASUREMENT (s1246, NOT a finding) — `npm run test:release` HAS ZERO AUTOMATED CALLERS, I RAN IT, AND ITS ONE RED IS A DOCUMENTED LOAD ARTIFACT THAT I RE-DERIVED AT THREE LOAD POINTS.** ✓ **The zero-caller claim re-derived, not inherited** (F-1126-2 said it): the only non-prose reference to `test:release` in the tree is **its own definition at `package.json:26`**. `scripts/deploy.sh` runs `npm run build`, **not** `build:release`, so `scripts/assert-release-build.mjs` never runs on the deploy path either. ⚠️ **One correction to F-1126-2's other half, by subject rather than by label:** it recorded `test:asset-diet`'s "only two references are comments telling a human to run it" — true of the **npm alias**, but `deploy.sh` invokes the **spec itself** through playwright (`e2e/asset-diet.spec.ts --grep "honest town and claim cues"`), so that guard does have a real automated caller. 🧪 **The run: 26 tests, `playwright.release.config.ts` (which builds `GR_RELEASE=e1 npm run build:release` and serves port 5190), rc=1, 69.3 s → 25 passed / 1 failed** — `release-build.spec.ts:185` *"later flagship URLs decline to the Claim"*, desktop-chrome, `expect(errors).toEqual([])` receiving one `THREE.GLTFLoader: Couldn't load texture blob:…`. 📉 **Load-monotonic, measured at three points on one unchanged tree, cited by TITLE not line:** **`--workers=1` → 6/6 PASS** · **workers=2 (the full 26-test run) → 1 fail** · **6 workers (`--repeat-each=3`) → 4 fail / 6, and in BOTH projects** — so my first run's "desktop-only" was a **sample, not a property** (the F-1222-3 shape, one fire after that lesson was written). ✓ **Already documented, which is why this is a measurement and not a discovery:** `logs/suite-red-inventory.md:611` records *"the release suite returned **25/26** with `release-build.spec.ts:185`, which re-measured **8/8 green in isolation**"* — my numbers reproduce it, and the mechanism is F-1180-2. **Nothing to fix; the release door is green under its own gate.** 🔻 **Residual worth one line for whoever wires CI:** `test:release` is the only gate over the E1 release door and it is still called by nothing — wiring it is the same open attended/owner call as F-1126-1, not a fire's to make.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:2960:✅ **F-1253-1 MEASURED + INSTRUMENTED s1253 (`9f8d11c9`, see the commit-hygiene note in F-1253-3) — I ASKED s1252'S QUESTION OF THE WHOLE TREE INSTEAD OF ONE GUARD, AND BUILT THE THING THAT ASKS IT AUTOMATICALLY.** 🧭 **WHY THIS ACT.** s1252 found `scripts/citation-title-guard.mjs` had no caller **by hand**, with a grep over `package.json` and `scripts/`, and closed with the lesson: *ask a guard not "does it pass?" but "is anyone asking it?"*. That guard had been RED for nine fires under nine green batteries. **Found by luck. The class fix is to make it findable by a battery**, so `scripts/gate-caller-audit.mjs` now resolves the caller graph and ratchets on it. 📊 **THE MEASUREMENT: 25 gate-shaped subjects, 5 ORPHANS**, all five grandfathered with a written reason in `scripts/gate-caller-baseline.json`. **Four are the release door, and it is one UNROOTED CHAIN:** nothing calls `test:release` → which is the only caller of `build:release` → which is the only caller of `scripts/assert-release-build.mjs`. Plus `test:asset-diet` and `test:preview`. ✓ **I RAN THEM RATHER THAN INFERRING, because s1252's orphan turned out to be red:** `test:asset-diet` **4/4 rc=0, 52.3 s**; `test:release --workers=1` **26/26 rc=0, 138.3 s** (its webServer runs `build:release`, so the release assert ran too). ➡️ **So the class is real but the exposure is LATENT, not live — and I say so rather than dressing a latent hole as a live one.** `verify:visual` is **not** a coverage hole: verified by `npx playwright test --list e2e/visual.spec.ts`, its 5 tests are collected by the default config (`playwright.config.ts` line 8, `testDir: './e2e'`), so `npm run test` already gates them. `scripts/anim-pass-dupecheck.mjs` is a forensic instrument, not a gate. 🔬 **THE INSTRUMENT'S OWN VOCABULARY WAS A CLAIM, AND MY FIRST DRAFT GOT IT WRONG (s1247, aimed at myself).** `build:release` is invoked by **nothing** in `package.json` or `scripts/` — its only caller is `webServer.command` in `playwright.release.config.ts`. A resolver reading only those two places reports it as an orphan **and is wrong**. `*.config.ts` is now in the edge vocabulary, written down in `EDGE_SOURCES`, and asserted by an arm. **Comments are stripped**, so the 40 lines of `run-guards.mjs` prose naming half the battery are not mistaken for edges. 🛡️ **IT REFUSES rather than greening over an unread subject (F-1251-2's class, FIFTH subject held to it).** A caller audit has a specially nasty vacuous mode: if the resolver silently fails to build the graph, **every** gate reads as an orphan — grandfather those once and it greens forever having resolved nothing. So it exits **2** when `git ls-files` fails, when `package.json` has no scripts block, when no `scripts/*.{mjs,sh}` is tracked, when the baseline is missing or unreadable, when no subject matches the gate patterns, **and when it cannot reach its own ANCHORS** — gates whose callers are structurally certain. 🧪 **15 ARMS, 15/15, each running the REAL script against a REAL git tree** (the subject is `git ls-files`, so a fake fs would test a different program). **Mutation-proved, subject restored byte-identical (sha256 `267da97664cb43aa`), 6 mutations → all 6 red: M1** drop the config edge **→ 2 · M2** stop stripping comments **→ 1 · M3** missing baseline read as `{}` **→ 1 · M4** remove the anchor self-check **→ 2 · M5** ratchet on every script not just gates **→ 2 · M6** green over an empty scripts block **→ 1.** 🪞 **AND ONE MUTATION SURVIVED FIRST TIME, WHICH IS THE MOST USEFUL THING THAT HAPPENED.** M2's first form disabled only *line*-comment stripping and **all 15 arms stayed green** — because the fixture's prose mention was **unquoted**, and an unquoted mention was never an edge. **The arm had been passing for the wrong reason.** The fixture now carries a quoted, matchable reference in **both** comment forms and asserts the guard FILE is orphaned too; M2 then reds exactly 1. ✅ **WIRED, GREEN ON MAIN FIRST:** `test:gate-callers` in `GUARDS` **and** `GATE_GUARDS`, **0.1 s**, because it gates what a drain itself writes — `package.json` and `scripts/**`. Four coupled roster assertions moved with it (`guards: 9/9`→`10/10`, base gate `4/4`→`5/5`, worker battery 7→8 rows, and the `GUARDS` list in `scripts/run-guards.test.mjs`). ⚖️ **§7.4 VETO WINDOW: delete `'test:gate-callers'` from `GATE_GUARDS` (or `GUARDS`) and the gating is undone.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:2964:⚠️ **F-1253-3 (s1253, OBSERVED — A PROCESS FINDING WITH TEETH) — THE ART RUNNER COMMITTED WITH A REPO-ROOT BROAD `add` AND SWALLOWED A LIVE FIRE'S ENTIRE IN-FLIGHT WORK INTO AN UNRELATED COMMIT.** `9f8d11c9` is titled **`runner(art): art-herald-class-engravings.md`** and contains, besides its 8 gazette raws and its LEDGER line: **`scripts/gate-caller-audit.mjs`, `scripts/gate-caller-baseline.json`, `package.json`, `scripts/run-guards.mjs`, all of `logs/session-scratch/s1253/`**, the health-watch churn, **and 4 regenerated `artifacts/asset-diet/*.png`** from my own probe run. **CLAUDE.md §4.2 is explicit: "Path-scoped `git add` only. Never `-A` at repo root. One concern per commit."** 🚨 **Two harms, and the second is the dangerous one.** (1) My work now sits under a message that does not describe it, and history rewriting is forbidden (§7.7), so this ledger entry is the only pointer — **that is why the hash above is an art commit.** (2) **The art batch's own drain diff is now contaminated with a guard slice**, so the next fire to drain `20260730-101334-art-herald-class-engravings.md` will see my files inside its scope — the Mistake #12 shape (gate contamination) arriving through the commit rather than through the tree. ➡️ **Nothing was lost** (verified: files on disk, wiring intact in all three files, tree clean). **Fix the CLASS: the art runner's commit step needs the same path-scoping every other writer in this factory obeys** — and until it does, no fire's uncommitted work is safe while an ART task finishes.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:3039:✅ **SHIPPED s1191 — `lane-c-eight-winds-rail-tough-row-settle.md` ACCEPTED** (merge `8b9e8af2b3ee88decf6ef2d2098204c880422865`, review `reviews/eight-winds-rail-tough-row-settle.md`; leaf → `merged`, `goal-tracker` 2/2). **THE PRE-DECLARED DECISIVE EXPERIMENT RAN, AND IT FLIPPED.** Masking the one-hand wrench moves `2v3` from **`0.706117` direct / `0.456002` mirrored (Δ +0.250115)** to **`0.705447` direct / `0.832448` mirrored (Δ −0.127001)**. 🔑 **The signature is ONE-SIDED — direct moves `−0.001` while mirrored gains `+0.376`** — which is exactly what the prop-confound predicted; a mask that merely shrank both scores would have proved nothing. Not a mask-size coin flip: **9/9** sensitivity cases (shaft 10/12/14 × jaw 20/22/24) stay mirror-dominant, Δ `−0.1176 … −0.1270`; subject self-mirror floor `0.4524952`. **Rows 2/3 are a lawful `nw`/`ne` mirror pair; the duplicate-row hypothesis is dead.** ✓ **EVERY PUBLISHED FIGURE RE-DERIVED BY THE DRAIN, NOT READ** — and **provenance proved by HASH**: the probe is deterministic across two runs and its live stdout is **byte-identical to the committed `probe-results.json`** (`bdb7d8c6…`), both locator boards matching their published SHA-256s. 🔑 **THE REASON IT PASSED THE GATE s1190 SET — IT VALIDATED ITS LOCATOR *AND* REJECTED A DISCRIMINATOR.** Rows 0/1 reproduce the known screen-left control before the instrument is aimed at the disputed rows; and the obvious cardinal cross-check **fails and is thrown out** — the consumer's `n` row 3 keeps the wrench screen-**left** (`−46.6 −49.8 −50.4 −46.8`) exactly like `s` row 0, so cardinal wrench side carries **no front/back information at all**. The report marks it `Confidence: NONE as a body-side control`, isolates it from the verdict, and changes nothing in the asset or contract. **A weaker run would have reported that agreement as corroboration.** GATES: `drain-block-check` ✅ CLEAR **first** · tsc 0 · build **1.58 s** asset-diet green · `test:node-guards` **61/61**. **No playwright, proportionate not thinned:** 39 files / **1110 insertions / 0 deletions**, and a **staged-path audit** (not the runner's assertion) confirms zero paths outside `artifacts/eight-winds-rail-tough/`, the probe and the run report — nothing under `src/`, `e2e/`, `assets/`, `scripts/` or any contract, so Mistake #10's honest answer is *"nowhere yet"*. Pure-add, all 39 LANE-TOUCHED-ONLY and disjoint from main's concurrent bookkeeping, no graft. 🔻 **F-1191-1 — the `nw`/`ne` LABELS are one tier below the PAIR, and this is the one claim a green gate cannot check.** "No diagonal row remains UNCERTAIN" must not be read as uniform certainty: what is HIGH-confidence is that rows 2/3 are **two distinct headings forming a mirror pair**. Which is `nw` and which is `ne` rests on the *visual* west/east read — **because the decisive instrument is symmetric by construction, masking the prop is precisely what removes the information that could tell the pair apart.** If wrong, the rows are swapped and the cure is a **one-line contract swap, no regeneration**. ➡️ the E2 wiring slice's in-game review should watch a **north-west walk on camera**; that settles the label more cheaply than any further pixel work. ⓘ **F-1191-2 — `logs/s1190-rail-tough-wrench-probe.mjs` is a genuinely re-runnable, deterministic, self-asserting instrument that NOTHING CALLS.** Deliberately **not** wired into `test:node-guards`: it reads `artifacts/` scratch extractions that are not part of the shipped asset set, and coupling the guard battery to scratch bytes is the wrong cure. 🎨 **NET: THE E2 ART BATCH IS NOW FULLY SIZED AT 4 ROWS AND SHIPS AS ONE REQUEST** — Coal Thief row 0 → `sw`; Steam Wrecker row 1 → lawful `se` **with a SINGLE cyan tank (F-1189-2, non-negotiable)** and row 2 → lawful `nw`; Rail Tough row 1 → one stable `se` (preserve `sw`/`nw`/`ne`). Exactly the request s1190 declined to spend a 75%-sized batch on.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:3040:- ✅ **SHIPPED s1193 (PARTIAL) — `art-e2-eight-winds-row-repairs.md`** (merge `f61843c074d71b93b6659784e034e7f654abf8ba`, review `reviews/eight-winds-e2-row-repairs.md`, LEDGER row **67**; leaf → `merged`; `goal-tracker` 2/2). **2 rows LANDED, 2 rows PARKED — and every published number was re-measured from the bytes rather than read.** ✓ **Independent row-hash proof over all 12 rows of the 3 sheets:** **10/12 byte-identical**, and the **only** rows that moved are **Steam Wrecker row 2** and **Rail Tough row 1** ⇒ the required **8 preserve rows are 8/8** and the batch ships **no regression**. 🔑 **THE CYAN INSTRUMENT WAS VALIDATED BEFORE IT WAS BELIEVED:** its first calibration read **30 px** where the run report’s published control said **116**, so by its own doctrine it was not trusted on the disputed row until it reproduced the known-good one; after calibration (teal predicate `g+b−2r ≥ 70`, dilate-then-cluster) it returns **1,1,1,1** with cores **111/109/105/106**, all screen-**LEFT**, on row 2 `nw` — reproducing the published `116/0, 112/0, 117/0, 99/0` — and **2,2,2,2** with cores `[43,84][60,90][36,90][39,83]` split **R+L** on row 1 `se`, matching the report exactly; **stable across dilation radii 4/6/8**, so not a knob-tuning artifact. `side:"L"` was **CONFIRMED** by the runner from **both** hemispheres of the shipped cardinal sheet (front `s` screen-right, back `n` screen-left ⇒ body-LEFT), satisfying **F-EW-5** — the root cause s1192 named (`cast.json` `props: []` ⇒ the builder emitted no prop-side clause at all) is now data. ⚠️ **THE PRE-DECLARED REJECT DID NOT FIRE, AND THAT IS THIS DRAIN’S JUDGEMENT CALL:** s1192 ruled “a **2** anywhere is a REJECT, not a note”, but row 1 reads `2,2,2,2` **byte-identical to its pre-batch state** — the gate assumed row 1 would be regenerated, whereas the run **rejected three bad takes and grafted none**, so the defect is **pre-existing, not shipped**. Rejecting would discard two good rows, would not fix row 1, and would punish exactly the behaviour the laws demand (**reject-don’t-stretch**, three **changed-premise** attempts per §7.5, **no failed take silently replaced** — all eight candidates retained under `reviews/eight-winds/crops/` with the reason in the filename). ✓ **Scope compliance checked against the MASTER, not against shorthand:** the Rail Tough row-1 graft is **explicitly commanded at master line 77** (criterion at line 87); NO-listed were Rail Tough rows **0/2/3** and the `nw`/`ne` **label** re-litigation — and rows 0/2/3 measure byte-identical. **GATES:** `drain-block-check` **CLEAR run FIRST** · tsc 0 · build **1.43 s** asset-diet · node guards **61/61** run **BEFORE** gating. **No playwright, and that is proportionate rather than a thinned bar — Mistake #10 answered from SOURCE:** runtime binds the **cardinal** cells (`src/assets/generated.ts:11-13`, `src/encyclopedia/registry.ts:18-20` → `assets/processed/char-*-sheet-walk4-a-r0c0.png`) while the **diagonal** `walkdiag4` raws this batch repaired are referenced **nowhere** in `src/` or `e2e/` and no processed cells shipped ⇒ nothing renders, and **GZ-01 is correctly NOT owed**. 🔻 **F-1193-2 (process, MEDIUM — benign here, live mechanism):** the commit also carries `tasks/lane-a-m3-05e-ledger-rush-card-evidence.md` — a master authored by **this fire** minutes earlier — plus two logs, none in the art TOUCH-ONLY list. **Neither runner path can produce that**, verified by reading `scripts/lane-runner-v3.sh`: the scoped art branch at `:88` is limited to `assets artifacts` **and is unreachable** because `dir_for_slot("art")` returns `worktrees/art` ≠ `$ROOT` (`:31`, guard `:78`), while the lane branch at `:105` runs with cwd `worktrees/art`, which **`.gitignore:13` excludes**, so its `-- .` pathspec stages nothing at root ⇒ **Codex committed to `main` itself**, against `AGENTS.md:18` and `:37`. It is **Mistake #3 with a working mechanism**: an ART run that commits broadly will sweep any concurrent uncommitted work — a fire’s or the attended session’s — into a commit attributed to an art task. **Cheapest durable fix: make `worktrees/art` a REAL git worktree, which also closes F-1120-2** (566.47 MB AT RISK) — both defects share that one root cause. 🔻 **F-1193-3 (residual, OPEN, not this batch’s fault):** Coal Thief row 0 is still the old duplicate `se`; Steam Wrecker row 1 is still `2,2,2,2`. **Re-queue ONLY with a changed generation premise** — three are already spent on each (base prompt · `--retake-mirrored` · retake + cardinal-front-row control) and §7.5 forbids an identical retry. ⓘ **F-1193-4:** all three raws exceed the 600 KB cap (1.20–1.37 MB), reported not optimized, consistent with LEDGER rows 63–65. **ART-SLOT LAW: AT RISK 748 files / 566.47 MB** (unchanged) · **LOCAL-ONLY 3 files / 2.70 MB** (was 0 at s1192 — one push from safe).
tasks/BACKLOG.md:3058:🔧 **F-1167-1 — RULED 2026-08-09, NOW WORK NOT A QUESTION (head flipped 🔺→🔧 s1615; the fork text below is kept verbatim as provenance): the ratified ADVANCE STREAM and eight lazy-GLB specs contradict each other, and one of them has to give.** Clustering the 303 reds showed **eight `town-*-blender` specs failing ONE assertion** (`expect(requests).toEqual([])`) at two sites each = **32 executions, ~10.6% of the whole red board**. ✓ **Mechanism found at source, and the data carried its own control:** two siblings — `town-dynamo-hall-blender` (12/12) and `town-stamp-mill-blender` (10/10) — are **fully green with the same assertion**, and they are **exactly** the two ids excluded at `src/town/TownTavernPilot.ts:49` (`.filter(([id]) => id !== 'stamp-mill' && id !== 'dynamo_hall')`) from `townPrefetchUrls()`, whose only caller is the idle warmer `src/assets/AdvanceStream.ts:30`. ✓ **Dates settle it:** all ten specs were added **2026-07-13**; the Advance Stream merged **2026-07-25 `e109639f`**, and `git log -S` proves **the two-id exclusion was introduced in that same commit** — someone kept two specs green and left eight red. ➡️ **FORK: (a) the prefetch is right** (it is a verbatim owner directive, BACKLOG:879) **⇒ the eight `toEqual([])` assertions are OBSOLETE and must be re-scoped to *mount* laziness — and then the two exclusions at `:49` are themselves the bug, silently denying the owner's warm-town benefit to two models to appease tests; **(b) pilot GLBs should not be prefetched** ⇒ extend the filter to all pilots and the eight reds are a real regression. **Recommendation (a).** **NOT fire-authored — §2E hard limit, this is a design fork.** ⚠️ **Do NOT fold in `asset-diet.spec.ts:101`** (`townResponseBytes < 25_000_000`, now red BOTH): BACKLOG:879 records it gating **4/4 with the prefetch live** on 07-25, so that budget held then and does not now — **a separate, real regression**, and the only evidence that could argue against (a). 🔑 The eight went red on 07-25 and nobody noticed for **three days**, because until this merge there was no machine-readable red list. ⚖️ **OWNER RULED 2026-08-09: "Prefetch wins" — branch (a): re-scope the eight `toEqual([])` specs to MOUNT-laziness and REMOVE the two-id exclusion at TownTavernPilot.ts:49 (stamp-mill + dynamo_hall get the warm-town benefit). FIRE-AUTHORABLE, one master; F-1532-2's walkthrough table unblocks behind it (author as-is once this merges).** ✅ **PRECONDITION DISCHARGED s1615 (2026-08-10) — THE CONTRADICTION IS STILL LIVE, REPRODUCED RATHER THAN INHERITED.** s1614 carried this item forward with an explicit caveat — *"MEASURE BEFORE AUTHORING: confirm those eight assertions are actually still present and still contradicting (I did not run that suite — it is a claim from F-1167-1's own text)"*. s1615 ran it: `e2e/town-tavern-blender.spec.ts:205` ("LITE tier always keeps the facade and never fetches the GLB") **FAILED** on main, desktop-chrome, `--workers=1`, received `["http://127.0.0.1:5188/assets/pilots/tavern-3d/town-v3-tavern.glb"]`; its sibling `:154` ("Tavern pilot is lazy, contract-valid, visual-only, and stays inside the frame-time gate") **PASSED in the same run** — the timing gradient this row's own blast radii predicted (81.8% vs 20.4%). 🔑 **NEW MECHANISM THIS ROW DID NOT RECORD, and it changes what a runner must NOT touch: the offending request is issued in the MENU phase, not the town phase.** `advanceStreamPriority` adds the town at priority 1 for `scene.kind==='menu'` (`src/assets/AdvanceStream.ts:62-63`), and the gate `threeDimensionalAssetsEnabled()` (`:250-252`) reads `window.location.search` **at enter time** — which is EMPTY on the specs' bare `await page.goto('/')` (all ten specs). The helper's later `history.replaceState` to `?terrain2d` / `tier=lite` correctly disables *subsequent* prefetch but cannot un-issue the menu's. ⚠️ **Therefore the `tier=lite` failures are NOT a lite-gate defect, and the specs' `?terrain2d` default and `!url.search` collector filter are NOT the bug** — a fire "fixing" the helper would suppress the prefetch and make the specs test a configuration no player is in, i.e. the opposite of the ruling. The cure is the ASSERTIONS, exactly as the owner ruled. 🆕 **AUTHORED: `f1615-1-prefetch-wins-mount-laziness` → lane-a, QUEUED** (scope covers **ten** specs, not eight — removing the `:49` exclusion newly exposes `stamp-mill` and `dynamo-hall`, which are green today only because they are excluded). 🔻 **DESK-DROPPED s1615: this is work with a ruling behind it, not a question awaiting a word.**
