tasks/BACKLOG.md:3352:🛑 **THE FAMILY HAS BEEN PLAYING A 23-HOUR-OLD BUILD, AND THE DEPLOY LOG SAID "DEPLOYED ok" (F-1049-1, s1049 2026-07-25, PROVEN BY ASKING THE LIVE SITE).** No decision needed from you — this is a heads-up plus one instruction: **do NOT run `wrangler login`.** Auth is fine and has been all day (5 CF API calls today, all HTTP 200, zero 401/403). What happened: the live site serves build `49dbce7a` from **2026-07-24T17:20Z**, so **174 commits never reached you — including the whole Opus-5 3D night (E9 + E10 town wardrobes, the Twin Banks braid, the deepwater wave counter).** `deploy.sh` believed wrangler's exit code; wrangler 4.107.0 crashed inside its own error-exit path (`process.exit('ENOENT')`) and exited **0** after publishing nothing, so a failed deploy was logged as a success. The real transport failure is an upload timeout on your slow line (`UND_ERR_HEADERS_TIMEOUT`, 8× in the last run against ~118MB), made worse because `deploy.sh` uploads from the live `dist/` that every fire's gate build rewrites underneath it. **Two correctives are moving:** `deploy-honest-and-serialized` (report the real error + serialize) was already running when this was found; `deploy-verify-published` (**success requires a published URL + a post-deploy `/version.json` confirmation**, upload from an immutable snapshot) is authored and queues after it merges. **Until one lands, treat every `DEPLOYED ok` in the log as unverified** — the honest check is `node`-fetching `https://gold-rush-3in.pages.dev/version.json` and comparing it to `git rev-parse --short=8 HEAD`. Evidence: `reviews/deploy-path-false-green.md`. *(Optional, your call only because it costs money/time on your line: wrangler 4.114.0 is available and may fix the exit-code bug — the URL check makes us safe either way, so there is no rush.)* ✅ **BOTH CORRECTIVES HAVE NOW LANDED (s1050, `00f5d464`) AND THE LYING HAS STOPPED — but you are still on the old build, and here is the one decision I need from you.** The proof is a real deploy I ran tonight: it failed at 15.4 minutes and **said so** (`deploy_failed`, empty URL) where yesterday's identical failure wrote `DEPLOYED ok`; the live site independently agrees (still `49dbce7a`, 2026-07-24). So the instrument is fixed and trustworthy — the transport is not. 🛑 **F-1050-3, THE THING NOBODY HAD MEASURED: the deploy uploads 423.4 MB in 3,054 files, not the "~118MB" every note has been repeating.** I measured `dist/` byte-by-byte. The 118MB was the asset-diet's *first-town runtime* number (93.8 + 24.3), which is a different question that got quietly reused as the upload size. **306.8 MB of it — 72% — is `.glb`.** That turns the timeouts from bad luck into arithmetic: 423 MB over your line vs a 300-second per-request timeout, dying at ~15.5 min every time, 9× `UND_ERR_HEADERS_TIMEOUT` in wrangler's own log tonight, **zero auth errors** (so `wrangler login` is still the wrong move). **THE CALL, and it is yours because it changes what ships to players:** shrink the upload rather than retry it. My recommendation — **(a)** stop shipping the undieted GLB originals if the dieted ones are what the game actually loads (406 `.glb` files ship; the diet only names 235), which alone could cut most of the 306 MB; or **(b)** move the heavy `.glb`/`.png` to R2/a CDN and keep Pages for the app shell. **(a) is the cheap experiment and I can have a fire measure exactly which GLBs the running game requests, if you say go.** Two words unblock it. Until then every deploy will keep failing honestly, which is better than succeeding falsely but still leaves the family on Friday's build. Evidence: `reviews/deploy-verify-published.md`. ✅ **s1051 MEASURED THE PREMISE, AND IT CHANGES THE ANSWER — YOUR DECISION IS NOW ARITHMETIC, NOT A GUESS. First, the correction: recommendation (a) as I wrote it CANNOT BE DONE.** There are no undieted originals to stop shipping — `scripts/asset-diet.mjs:76` writes each optimized model **back over itself** (`io.write(file, document)`), so the 406 `.glb` in `dist/` are 406 **distinct models**; deleting any removes something the game loads. The "406 ship, diet names 235" line was true but I read it as duplicates, and it is not (**F-1051-1**, my error, corrected before it cost you anything). **The real lever is one line up, in the SELECTOR: 171 of the 406 GLBs (217.3 MB) were never ELIGIBLE for optimization at all** — `asset-diet.mjs:41-52` names only `*-terrain.glb`, `/landmarks/` and 7 town models, then matches dist by `startsWith(name + '-')`. **45 of those 171 are era variants of the very buildings it already compresses, missed on one character — the convention is `schoolhouse.e8-…` and the selector tests `schoolhouse-…`, so the dot excludes them** (**F-1051-2**). The proof needs no transform: shipped `schoolhouse-…glb` is **224 KB** (dieted) while `schoolhouse.e2…e10` are **1.47–1.83 MB each**. **I then measured rather than projected — all 171 run through the diet's EXACT transform on copies (`dist/` never written), 171/171 succeeded, zero failures: 217.30 MB → 24.39 MB, an 89% cut, 192.9 MB saved** (bucket A, the 45 era variants: 66.65→10.35 MB / 84%; bucket B, the other 126: 150.65→14.04 MB / 91%; biggest single win `ark-plaza-e10` **5.81→0.42 MB**). **That takes the upload from 423.4 MB to ~230.5 MB — a 46% cut, with zero change to which assets exist.** 🔎 **AND A SECOND, LARGER FINDING YOU SHOULD SEE BEFORE YOU CHOOSE (F-1051-3): the deploy publishes ALL TEN EPOCHS.** `deploy.sh:48` runs `npm run build`, **not** `build:release` — and this repo already owns an E1 release guard, `assert-release-build.mjs:40`, written to **fail** a release containing any `e2..e10` asset. Applying that same rule to what actually shipped: **418 files, 205.6 MB — 49% of the upload — is later-epoch content** (171.8 MB GLB, 15.5 webp, 13.6 png, plus 3 era music loops at 4.7 MB). **THE CALL, now with numbers on both options:** **(1) FIX THE SELECTOR — my recommendation, and it needs one word.** Measured −192.9 MB, no change to what players can reach, and it applies the transform you already ratified to more of the same asset class. **(2) SHIP E1-ONLY** — −205.6 MB, bigger, but it **removes the deep-saga epochs from the deployed build**, and the T6–T10 saga wall only just shipped (s958), so that is a product decision and yours alone. The two overlap heavily; **(1) is the one with no product consequence.** **Honest limits, so you are not oversold:** I did **not** render a visual side-by-side for the 171 (the transform is owner-accepted for this asset class and these are the same buildings whose base models are already dieted, but that is an argument, not a screenshot); I did **not** prove ~230 MB *completes* where 423 MB timed out — halving is the largest measured lever, not a guarantee; and I did **not** test which of the 171 the running game actually requests, so *dropping* may beat *compressing* for some. **A fire did not just fix it because it changes bytes players receive and you have an open question on exactly this — one word on (1) and it is a lane task the same day.** Full evidence + per-bucket tables: `reviews/glb-census.md`; probes retained in `logs/_s1051_*.mjs`. ✅ **s1056 MEASURED OPTION (2) THE SAME WAY, AND IT IS MUCH BIGGER THAN I TOLD YOU — plus it makes option (1) moot. Option (2) is not −205.6 MB / 49%; it is −350.0 MiB / 82.7%.** I stopped subtracting from a file listing and **built the thing**: `GR_RELEASE=e1 npm run build:release` → **1,695 files, 76,962,747 bytes (73.4 MiB)** against the all-epoch **3,053 files / 423.4 MiB**, measured on the same tree one build apart. My 49% projection only subtracted later-epoch *assets*; it could not see that the E1 build also emits a smaller code bundle and 407 fewer images. **The all-epoch row independently reproduces F-1050-3 exactly (423.4 MiB, 3,053 files, 406 GLB / 306.8 MiB)** by a different method, which is the cheapest proof both numbers count the right thing. **TWO CORRECTIONS TO WHAT I PUT ON YOUR DESK: (i) option (2) SUBSUMES option (1) — they are not additive.** After E1-only only **53 GLBs / 19.5 MiB** remain and **all 53 are already dieted**, so the selector fix has nothing left to bite on; (2) beats (1) by ~157 MiB *and then leaves it with ~nothing to do*. **(ii) the dominant class flips: in an E1-only build IMAGES are 759 files / 47.1 MiB = 64%**, against 19.5 MiB of GLB — every note in this saga has pointed at `.glb`, and past E1-only that is the wrong target. **AND IT IS NOT A PROPOSAL — IT IS A WORKING ARTIFACT TODAY: builds in 6s, its own guard passes (`[release-build] E1-only: 1695 files, 76962747 bytes`), and `npm run test:release` is 24/24 GREEN both projects** — including a full first-player run (*places a Dry Gulch spring sluice*, 34.5s), all five E1 maps booting, debug/era seams proven inert, later-flagship URLs declining to the Claim, a forged locked launch declining, an imported later ledger healing and playing. **STILL YOURS AND UNSHIPPED, deliberately:** it removes the deep-saga epochs the T6–T10 wall only just shipped — that is a product call, §7.3, and measuring it is not choosing it. **Honest limit: I did NOT deploy it.** That 73.4 MiB completes where 423.4 MiB times out is arithmetic (~23 min vs ~128 min at your measured ~3.3 MB/min), the largest measured lever in this saga — **not a guarantee**. Full evidence: `reviews/e1-release-build-measured.md`; probes `logs/_s1056_*.mjs`. **One word on (2) and the family is playing the current build the same day.** ✅ **s1057 — NO DECISION NEEDED, one line so you know the story is now closed on BOTH scripts (F-1057-1, FIXED + PROVEN).** While auditing the deploy guard's never-exercised success path I found that everything we fixed about honest deploy reporting had been applied to `scripts/deploy.sh` and **never to `scripts/deploy-site.sh`** — the *second* wrangler-deploying script in this repo, the one that publishes the marketing site and the Gazette news page. It carried the same F-1049-1 disease in a **stronger** form: it believed wrangler's exit code (`:39`), it *formatted away* a missing URL into the friendly literal `'(url in log)'` and still logged `DEPLOYED ok` (`:41`), and it grepped the URL out of the **cumulative** log with `tail -1` — so a run that published **nothing** would hand back a **previous run's URL** as its own. **I did not deduce this, I reproduced it:** restored the pre-fix script from git, ran it against a stubbed wrangler that exits 0 and publishes nothing, and watched it print `DEPLOYED ok '(url in log)'`; then with a polluted log, `DEPLOYED ok https://s1057-stub.pages.dev` — **a URL from an earlier run, for a deploy that never happened.** Fixed script says `FAILED` in both, and still says `DEPLOYED ok` when a real URL is published. **Severity was LATENT and I want that stated plainly rather than dressed up: `logs/deploy-site.log` did not exist and `agenttown.pages.dev` does not resolve ⇒ it had never run for real, so no false green ever reached you, and it has no automated caller.** I fixed it anyway because F-1055-1's standing order is *"if you find a third such gap, fix the class, not the instance"* — and the class is now **verifiably closed**: exactly two scripts in this repo invoke `wrangler …deploy`, and both are cured. **The audit's other three answers were all POSITIVE, measured not assumed:** the verify path's build-id comparison is **format-compatible** (live serves `49dbce7a`, 8 chars; `PUBLISHED_BUILD` is `--short=8`) so it is not silently doomed to report `deploy_unverified` forever; the asset-budget guard is **NOT vacuous** (proven by real logged output — `16381249 bytes, 8618751 headroom`, both projects — *not* an F-1044-1/F-1047-1/F-1052-1 repeat); and `lockf` exists, so the lock preamble does not silently no-op every deploy. **Honest limits:** `VERIFY attempt` has never once appeared in `logs/deploy.log` because no deploy has ever succeeded, so the success path remains proven only against stubs; and whether s1053's deliberate 3×15s alias-promotion window is *sufficient* is unvalidated — if it is short, a real success reports a **false red**, which is the safe direction. Evidence: `reviews/deploy-site-false-green.md`. **Deploy staleness refreshed by asking the live site: still `49dbce7a`, now 220 commits behind.** ✅ **s1059 — NO DECISION NEEDED, and it is the last line of this story (F-1059-1, FIXED + PROVEN).** s1057 closed the false-green class in both scripts; I asked the question that sentence does not answer — **who is watching it stay cured?** Nobody was. `test-deploy-contract.sh` copies and runs **`deploy.sh` alone**, so the sibling s1057 had just fixed had **zero** automated coverage; and that test had **no executable caller anywhere** (not in `package.json`, not in any script or CI — only a ledger leaf). That is not hypothetical rot: `goals.json` already records F-1052-1, where this very test *"has been RED since `00f5d464`… two consecutive deploy correctives shipped without their own contract test ever being run — the same unrun-guard family as F-1049-1 / F-1047-1 / F-1044-1."* **Landed:** a new `scripts/test-deploy-site-contract.sh` (7 cases) plus npm wiring for **both** guards. **The proof is a mutation control, and it discriminates:** pointed at the pre-fix script it FAILS 5 assertions — including *"re-served an EARLIER run's URL as its own"* — while still passing `wrangler-red`, `real-url` and both skips, so it is not blanket-failing whatever it is handed; pointed at the current script, 7/7 green. **Two new cases go beyond s1057's hand-run four:** a polluted log must not suppress a *genuine* success (guards the fail-closed direction), and the `pages project create` banner URL — printed for a project merely *reserved* — must never be reported as a publish. **Bonus, and it discharges a limit s1053 wrote about itself:** I **executed** the pre-existing deploy contract test for the first time (s1053 could only read it — bash is gated for fires; node is not) — **13